"In casual Camouflage pants Skillful military trend and urban chic shake hands. With the patterned pants you can make simple outfits in a jiffy more interesting and ensure a successful eye-catcher.\nThe origin of today\u2019s trendy camouflage pattern goes back to the First World War. At that time the soldiers wore the brown and green shades to camouflage. Today, however, the pattern has long arrived in the fashion world. The eye-catching trousers are especially a great trend for leisure.\nCamouflage pants are available in numerous fits and lengths. For all, the right combination is the be-all and end-all. So the look looks more harmonious if you pick up one of the trouser colors \u2013 often beige, brown and green \u2013 in the rest of the garments. For the trendy allover military look you can Camouflage pants to wear Desert Boots and Parka. Discreet is the combination with a sporty Serafino shirt and casual sneakers. Skinny jeans or even leggings with camouflage pattern are the perfect combo partners for colorful city looks. They fit perfectly with plateau sneakers, hoodie and trucker cap, for example."
"Have you ever spotted beautiful place mats in the store and wished you had a reason to buy them. Grab those place mats because they make beautiful throw pillows that take minutes to make! These are seriously the easiest pillows you can possibly make.\nI have had these place mats in our cupboards for about 5 years. I got them because I thought they where pretty, but we honestly never once used them. With a two and four year old in the house, if I did put them on the table, I found myself picking them up off the floor thirty times a day. They are just to pretty to actually use at dinner time. The don\u2019t deserve to be covered in tomato soup. I was cleaning out my cabinets this week and decide it was time to make these into something that we could use \u2013 throw pillows!\nThis is so simple you have to try this. Make sure you are using place mats that are double sided. Mine are reversible and have two different patterns. This will not work if the place mats aren\u2019t double sided. Here is how to turn that place mat into a pillow.\nTake a seam ripper and open the seam of the place mat on one side about 4 inches (big enough for you to put the stuffing in). My mats had a tag on them so I pulled the seam apart where the tag was sewed in to get rid of it.\nUse your fiberfill to stuff the pillows to you liking. I had one large bag that I was able to fill all four pillows with and still have some left. Now, all you need to do is close the seam back up and you are all done. These take about 5 minutes to make from start to finish if you are using a sewing machine. So fast and so simple. You could totally do this by hand if you don\u2019t have a sewing machine too.\nPush all of your stuffing to one side of the pillow \u2013 this makes it much easier to pin the hem back together in a nice straight line.\nNow just sew the opening that you made back together. Use the same color thread that your place mat already has so that it blends right in. You can literally just follow the stitch that is already on the place mat. Make sure you back stitch to secure, and remove your pins. Done! Now fluff your pillow so the stuffing is evened out and put your beautiful new 5 minute throw pillows out to enjoy.\nI absolutely adore the off white side of these. It has a little lace trim that is so simple and pretty. These are a great size for the couch, a bench, the bed, a chair, or a bed as decorative pillows.\nIf you have a hard time finding throw pillows that match your decor and that you really love \u2013 check the dinning section for place mats next time you are shopping. I promise this is as easy as it looks."
