"Xi-anna Graham, a 24-year-old mother of four, was strangled at her Marietta, Georgia home by her live-in boyfriend, police say. At least one of her children told authorities they heard a \"loud commotion.\"\nXi-anna Graham was doing what she could. She attended classes at West Georgia Technical College to make something of herself and provide for her family.\nGraham, 24, was raising four of her children in suburban Atlanta with love and affection. But her live-in boyfriend was a hot head, police say.\nTwenty-seven-year-old Christopher Gene Scarboro had several run-ins with authorities this year alone. On July 4, a warrant obtained by Atlanta TV station WXIA indicates he allegedly struck a man in the face. Months before that, on May 27, allegedly headbutted another woman.\nOn Wednesday, August 22, 2018, law enforcement doing a welfare check on a home in Marietta, Georgia found Graham unresponsive on the floor, according to media reports. At least one of the victim's children was reported to be in another part of the home at the time. The child told authorities they heard a \"loud commotion,\" according to WXIA-TV.\nPolice say they were called to a home on Bonnie Dell Drive for a welfare check Just before 3:30 a.m. Wednesday. When they entered, police said they found a woman who had been strangled in an act of domestic violence.\nDetectives said Graham was allegedly strangled by her live-in Scarboro. He has been charged with multiple counts, including homicide-murder and aggravated assault.\n\"Our hearts and thoughts and prayers go out to everyone involved in this tragedy,\" Marietta police spokesperson Chuck McPhilamy told local media outlets. \"If you're the one watching this and you know someone who may be in a situation that they shouldn't be and that it could be an abusive situation that they shouldn't be, please the life you could be saving could be theirs.\"\nSee more about domestic violence as well as our Resources Page."
"Everybody loves a lazy Sunday, right? A long lie-in, newspapers in bed (or your tablet, of course, if you are more technically minded) and perhaps a trip out in the afternoon or taking that opportunity to binge-watch your favourite Netflix series.\nOf course, all of this is only possible if you don&apos;t work on Sundays - which, of course, a lot of people do. Weekend work is par for the course for certain industries, such as retail, hospitality or the emergency services (and journalism!).\nAnd some people love it or, at least, it suits them - perhaps because it fits in with their lifestyle or because they like having time off during the week.\nThere will be instances, however, when you may need to spend time with your family, or ease up on the amount of shifts you&apos;re doing.\nThis is, potentially, where one little known law could come in handy and the Mirror Online has looked into it.\nWorkers in certain industries don&apos;t actually have to work on Sundays - and can opt out if they so choose.\nAgain, all staff should be have been told about these Sunday working rights when they first started work.\nLooking for a change of career? Of course, we at the Derby Telegraph always want to help you find a new job if you&apos;re looking and you can look, any time, at our dedicated Fish4Jobs website for new vacancies.\nAn original version of this story has appeared on the Mirror Online."
"Kodak Black in court on April 26, 2017 in Fort Lauderdale, Fla.\nKodak Black recently celebrated completing his required anger management courses but now, his celebration has been cut short. On Monday (Oct. 9), Black was indicted on first-degree sexual conduct charges by a grand jury in Florence County, South Carolina.\nBlack, born Dieuson Octave, was arrested for allegedly sexually assaulting a woman back in February 2016 at the Comfort Inn & Suites around the time the rapper was set to perform at a local nightclub. WPDE reports that the woman first reported the incident to a school nurse who then presented the allegations to the Florence County Sheriff\u2019s office.\nThe Florida native is prohibited from commenting on the situation whether in print, digital, or his social media platforms and is not allowed to contact the victim nor the victim\u2019s family."
