"On Sunday the Crew Team raced at the 5th and final Manny Flick regatta of the season and continued to make progress while experimenting with line-ups and race plans. The Freshmen team continued to demonstrate speed with good results in the Novice Eight and Freshman Eight Time Trials while the Varsity Eight also improved on the previous week\u2019s results with a solid race under very fast conditions on the Schuylkill River. The team broke its Second Varsity Eight into a Varsity and JV Four which competed valiantly against a large field in each event.\nThanks to everyone who came to Philadelphia to cheer on the team on the banks of the Schuylkill River and to our hard-working parents for once again hosting an outstanding operation providing food for all our athletes and their families and friends during and after the racing.\nIn addition, a continuing thanks to everyone who has supported the team\u2019s fundraising effort for the spring break trip."
"Bronchitis is an inflammation of the lining of your bronchial tubes, which carry air to and from your lungs. Bronchitis may be either long-term or acute. A more severe affliction, chronic bronchitis, is a persistent irritation or inflammation of the lining of the bronchial tubes, often on account of smoking. Chronic bronchitis is among the conditions included in chronic obstructive pulmonary disease (COPD). :D.\nUnderstanding Treatment of Bronchitis Evaluations are often not necessary in the case of acute bronchitis, as the disease is generally not difficult to discover through your description of symptoms and a physical exam. In cases of chronic bronchitis, the doctor will likely get a X ray of your chest to check the extent of the lung damage, as well as pulmonary function tests to quantify how well your lungs are working. In some cases of chronic bronchitis, oral steroids to reduce inflammation and supplemental oxygen may be necessary. In healthy individuals viral bronchitis mucus (http://bronch.forumotion.com/t87-viral-bronchitis-mucus-what-are-the-real-causes) have no long-term health problems and regular lungs, are generally not necessary. Your lungs are exposed to illnesses if you have chronic bronchitis."
"Ever since we moved into our house the small bedroom has always been the \"baby\" room. This means that the original Winnie the Pooh bedding, curtains etc have never been changed. Over time this bedroom has also become the junk storage room, I was beginning to forget what the original space looked like.\nOne of my goals this year was to make a few home improvements and I thought the best place to begin was Layla's room seen as she is no longer my baby, closer to a toddler these days.\nYou can see from the picture above that we couldn't even shut the drawer and the wardrobe properly as it was filled to the brim.\nLayla's favourite character of recent months has been Minnie Mouse so we decided to give her bedroom a Minnie makeover. We had already made a start before Christmas, I'm sure you can see Minnie peeking through the clutter.\nLots of changes were made, none of them major but I feel they made a huge difference.\n* We swapped the cot and changer round which opened up the room a bit more.\n* Curtains, bedding and light shade now completely Minnie.\n* I added a book corner, complete with my cuddly elephant that is a young 28 years old himself!!\n* The changer is now clutter free and in its place the very handy bunny night light and of course Minnie mouse to watch over Layla.\nWith the Minnie makeover complete, I was sad to see all of the Winnie the Pooh removed from the room, reminded me that my girls really are all growing up however it is nice to see that Layla now has a proper space of her own to not only sleep in but play in too.\nHow are your children's rooms decorated, do they have a particular theme??"
