"The sauna heater is the heart of the sauna room. Staying true to the company\u2019s deep Finnish roots, the Finlandia FIN-30 sauna heater generates an intensely penetrating heat. The FIN-30 is perfect for powering sauna rooms up to 130 adjusted cubic feet (ACF).\nThe FIN-30 heater features durable stainless-steel elements for years of exceptional performance and maintenance-free operation. The unit is designed so that the rocks are in direct contact with the heating elements for greater efficiency. Its large rock capacity ensures a more even and consistent heat.\nThe FIN 30 has a power rating of 3 kilowatts. The unit is available in 240V, single-phase or 208V, three-phase configurations. The heater may be ordered with the standard red finish or the optional stainless-steel exterior (FIN-30-S).\nFIN Series sauna heaters require the use of a wall-mounted control for operation. Located outside the sauna room, the control allows bathers to adjust features such as bathing duration (1-60 minutes) and temperature. Select the available control that best suits your preferences.\nThe FIN 30 heater carries a limited lifetime warranty and, unlike cheap imitations, is fully serviceable. FIN series electric sauna heaters are UL and ETL listed for unmatched safety and quality.\nFor sauna rooms up to 130 cu. ft."
"For other applications and installers, they\u00e2\u20ac\u2122re either an MSI wrapped in a single executable (like Acrobat Reader), or a different type of installer (like Nullsoft Installer) and will have their own switches.\u00c2 Below are a few of the tricks and tools I use to find out how best to get these applications into \u00e2\u20ac\u0153silent\u00e2\u20ac\u009d mode as much as possible.\nElevated Shortcut\u00c2 to give the ability to run certain apps without UAC or administrator capabilities on a \u00e2\u20ac\u0153per shortcut\u00e2\u20ac\u009d basis, can upgrade an existing shortcut to have admin rights, or create a completely new one.\nSet to snapshot so you can rollback often, nothing but updates installed so you can install all/one app and then roll it back if it works as expected, leaving a clean machine for further testing.\nIf you can, right-click and \u00e2\u20ac\u0153WinRar\u00e2\u20ac\u009d or \u00e2\u20ac\u0153UniExtract to Subdir\u00e2\u20ac\u009d the installer into a folder, and find the setup.exe or similar in there, and return to Option #1 to see if you can run a silenced install.\nThis is especially true for applications like Chrome and Shockwave Player that have small \u00e2\u20ac\u0153helper\u00e2\u20ac\u009d applications for download, but then pull down the \u00e2\u20ac\u0153real\u00e2\u20ac\u009d installer and run that.\u00c2 If a 40MB app has a 300K installer, you know something\u00e2\u20ac\u2122s up.\nWatch out for these, as they will often delete the \u00e2\u20ac\u0153helper\u00e2\u20ac\u009d app once the full installer\u00e2\u20ac\u2122s been downloaded, thus killing your automation by removing the installer\u00e2\u20ac\u2122s executable.\nOnce you\u00e2\u20ac\u2122ve got that folder, look for executables and drop them on the USSF again, to see what you can find.\nOption #4: Is there a portable version?\nSometimes there\u00e2\u20ac\u2122s no graceful way to install an application, but if you still want to install it quickly to a number of workstations, see if perhaps the developers offer a \u00e2\u20ac\u0153portable\u00e2\u20ac\u009d version of the app.\u00c2 One that runs completely out of the installation folder, without any installation necessary.\nNote: Portable applications are not listed as installed applications in the registry, so they\u00e2\u20ac\u2122re effectively \u00e2\u20ac\u0153invisible\u00e2\u20ac\u009d to utilities that search systems for installed programs\u00c2 Keep that in mind if you plan on going portable with something \u00e2\u20ac\u201c you probably won\u00e2\u20ac\u2122t be able to track it effectively.\nFonts can be copied into the target system\u00e2\u20ac\u2122s \u00e2\u20ac\u0153c:\\Windows\\Fonts\\\u00e2\u20ac\u009d folder or app by hand if you\u00e2\u20ac\u2122re an admin, but if you forcibly copy them in via network access (\\\\machinename\\c$\\windows\\fonts\\), they won\u00e2\u20ac\u2122t show up as usable until they\u00e2\u20ac\u2122re been imported into the registry too.\nOnce you have your installer figured out, you can add the executable to the Windows Post Installation tool (WPI) found in the \\WindowsUpdates folder.\nAfter launching the WPI, click\u00c2 Config, and find the application you\u00e2\u20ac\u2122re replacing, or updating.\nEg: New version of Firefox installation that takes 4 seconds to install is\u00c2 good\u00c2 \u00e2\u20ac\u201c but one that blows away your saved passwords and settings is\u00c2 bad.\nRun every app at least once to make sure it really did what you think it did \u00e2\u20ac\u201c It may have completed installation successfully, but does it actually\u00c2 launch\u00c2 now?\nExample: Office 2007 upgraded to Office 2010 can remove Access 2007, even if Office 2010 didn\u00e2\u20ac\u2122t include it.\nAlso watch out for installers/updates that forcibly reboot your system when completed silently.\nIs the application installed for ALL users, or just the currently logged-in user/admin?\u00c2 Google Chrome is a good example of this.\nTry not to leave icons all over your user\u00e2\u20ac\u2122s desktop \u00e2\u20ac\u201c collect them into a folder under c:\\users\\public\\desktop so they can\u00e2\u20ac\u2122t delete them, but can copy them out onto their own desktop or quicklaunch bar if they want to."
"Bang & Olufsen just opened The Music Box, a new immersive pop-up in SoHo. The temporary boutique consists of a collection of interactive vignette photo-ops, all of which help to shed light on the elevated musical dimensions of Bang & Olufsen, as well as allow for the visitor to be immersed in the B&O listening experience.\nUpon entering The Music Box, visitors are invited to enjoy the Bronze Collection, an exclusive colorway that unites Bang & Olufsen\u2019s best multiroom speakers in one space. Emblazoned with a giant sound-generating pendulum, users are able to listen to musical notes through a Beoplay A9 speaker. But the highlight of The Music Box is housed in the rear, which contains the B&O jazz club, in every sense of the word \u2014 dimly lit, curtain-lined, and showcasing the newly released Late Night Blue Collection. The collection is a nod at the old-school iconic jazz clubs of New York \u2013 except maybe minus the Beoplay A1 Bluetooth speaker and the Beoplay E8 wireless earphones, which are 100% present in this spot.\nOther interactive parts of the musical oasis include a brightly colored room displaying the new Beosound Edge, the award-winning wireless speaker (which features the world\u2019s first active bass port), and a cozy living room that shows how the state-of-the-art Beolab 90 loudspeakers (which boast more than 16,000 watts of acoustic power) can easily fit into any living space.\nThe Music Box by Bang & Olufsen is open now at 121 Spring Street."
