"MILTON, Mass. \u2013 Junior Zel Kerkel (Loudon, N.H.) made a career-high 19 saves but Plymouth State University fell to Curry College, 11-7, in a non-conference men's lacrosse game at Walter M. Kratz Field on Thursday afternoon.\nWith the setback the Panthers fall to 1-2, while Curry evens its record at 2-2.\nIn a back-and-forth game, the Colonels struck for six straight goals between the third and fourth quarters to take control. PSU had pulled in front, 6-5, after freshman Connor Delea (Manchester, N.H.) notched his second of the game, and sixth goal in the last two games, with 4:20 left in the third. Curry responded with the next six tallies, three each in the third and fourth quarters, before PSU tacked on a final goal in the last minute of play to account for the 11-7 final.\nThe Colonels took the lead midway through the opening quarter when junior MJ Urbano (Wakefield, Mass.) scored at the 7:39 mark. PSU countered with a pair of goals to take a 2-1 lead after the opening 15 minutes. Junior Paul Luongo (Northborough, Mass.) scored his second of the year to tie the game with 3:00 left in the quarter, and junior Jake Burns (Salem, N.H.) added his second of the year to give the Panthers the lead.\nCurry scored twice in the first five minutes of the second quarter to pull back in front, 3-2, but Delea tied the game with his first goal of the afternoon with 4:08 left in the half. Urbano gave the Colonels a 4-3 lead at the break when he scored with just under three minutes left in the half.\nSophomore Griffin Jacobson (Windham, Maine) tied the game for the Panthers 4:55 into the third quarter, but sophomore Jake Stebbins (Colchester, Vt.) gave the home team a one-goal advantage five minutes later. Luongo netted his second of the game to even the score once again, and Delea's goal 12 seconds later gave Plymouth State the 6-5 lead, setting up Curry's run to pull away.\nJacobson and Luongo each tallied a pair of goals, while Delea added two goals and an assist. Burns chipped in with a goal and an assist. Sophomore Charlie Harrington (Walpole, Mass.) and senior Kyle Stockman (Ballston Lake, N.Y.) led the Panthers with four ground balls each, while senior Lucas McCue (Brunswick, Maine) scooped up three. Junior Casey Elkins (Stow, Mass.) forced a team-high three Colonels' turnovers. Kerkel made a career-high 19 stops but suffered the loss to fall to 1-2.\nUrbano led all scorers with three goals for Curry, as senior Tom Cashin (Boxford, Mass.) added two goals and two assists. Cashin also led all players with six ground balls. Junior Josh Teller (Milford, Conn.) made 13 saves.\nCurry outshot the Panthers 41-29 for the game, with most of the difference coming in the second quarter as the home team fired 15 shots to PSU's five.\nPlymouth State travels to Castleton University on Sunday (Mar. 18) for a 1:00 p.m., non-conference game with the Spartans."
"Isn\u2019t this time of year exciting? We are learning all about the 2019 models and Chevrolet does not disappoint! They made some big changes to the Silverado for 2019. Here are just a few of the exciting changes, you can learn about all the changes for the Silverado and all of Chevrolet\u2019s models at Len Stoler Chevrolet.\nThe 2019 Silverado 1500 is bigger and lighter than its predecessor, with a wheelbase that\u2019s 3.9 inches longer than the 2018 version. Overall length is up as well, with the new truck being 1.6 inches longer than before.\nPassengers rejoice! The Silverado\u2019s larger footprint improves rear-seat comfort in the four-door crew cab, legroom grows from 40.9 inches to 43.8 inches. Along with the additional leg room, Chevy also added two large storage bins in the rear seatbacks, as well as storage space under the rear seat bottoms.\nA new roll-formed, high-strength steel bed floor is 1.5 times the strength of the previous one and has 12 stationary tie-downs that are double the strength of the ones on the previous truck. Another set of nine removable tie-downs will be offered as an accessory.\nThe 2019 Silverado will have a choice of six different powertrain combinations. Two new gasoline V-8 engines, a 5.3-liter unit and a 6.2-liter, all this means improved efficiency by way of General Motors\u2019 new Dynamic Fuel Management system, which can cut fuel flow to between one and seven cylinders as conditions permit.\nWith a steeper windshield and roof spoiler, the new Silverado has an aerodynamic efficiency despite riding an inch higher than previous models.\nCustom trims are available on all models of the 2019 Silverado making it easy to customize your new ride and give it a little personality!\nThese are just a few of the changes you\u2019ll see in the 2019 Chevy Silverado. For a complete list of changes and for all the custom trims and accessories connect with Len Stoler Chevrolet. They can help you customize the Chevy of your dreams and set you up to be one of the first ones to test drive a 2019 model when they arrive in the showroom."
