"Are Your Dry Eyes Related to Your Menstrual Cycle?\nUpdate: Researchers in Thailand have published the results of a recent trial which studied the effect of hormone therapy for dry eye syndrome in post-menopausal women.\nUnfortunately, after three months of hormone therapy, this group did not demonstrate improvement in dry eye symptoms over placebo.\nVersura P, Fresina M, Campos EC. Ocular surface changes over the menstrual cycle in women with and without dry eye. Gynecol Endocrinol. 2007 Jul;23(7):385-90.\nPiwkumsribonruang N, Somboonporn W, Luanratanakorn P, Kaewrudee S, Tharnprisan P, Soontrapa S. Effectiveness of hormone therapy for treating dry eye syndrome in postmenopausal women: a randomized trial. J Med Assoc Thai. 2010 Jun;93(6):647-52."
"As of recent, Koei Tecmo revealed news pertaining to their upcoming games\u2019 Western release dates. This spans across games like Toukiden 2, Atelier Firis: The Alchemist of the Mysterious Journey, Nights of Azure 2, and Samurai Warriors: Sanada Maru. Toukiden 2 released over in Japan on July 28th, and is currently available for PS3, PS4 and PS Vita.\nWestern fans looking forward to the popular demon hunting RPG game, Toukiden, will find that the second installment in the series will launch early 2017. As of course, this was announced by Koei Tecmo \u2014 who also revealed other titles heading Westward.\nIf you aren\u2019t familiar with Koei Tecmo and Omega Force\u2019s game, Toukiden 2 takes players on an Oni-hunting ride that\u2019s open world and filled with all sorts of strange characters. The second installment also features exploration, a town to visit and caves that hold dangerous Oni demons.\nWhile taking on the mantle of a Slayer, players will be in charge of defending humanity\u2019s last village from the ferocious demons lurking the fields. Each of these demons call for players to exercise different weapons to exterminate them and rid the land of dangerous threats.\nAs of now, the game isn\u2019t set to release on any platforms yet for the West. This means that it could potentially gain a PC version early 2017, which would be pretty cool. However, it has been noted by Koei Tecmo that the release date, as well as additional information, will drop in the following weeks, so I\u2019m sure that will happen in the mid-to-late part of this month.\nToukiden 2 released over in Japan two months ago on July 28th, and is currently available for PS3, PS4 and PS Vita. For more information on this game you can head on over to koeitecmoeurope.com.\nAnd if the above is not enough, you can watch some actual gameplay footage below courtesy of YouTuber Havian."
"A new type of online product image, developed by researchers at the University of Cambridge in collaboration with global consumer goods company Unilever, could improve the mobile shopping experience for the world\u2019s 2.5 billion smartphone users.\nWe want to improve the e-commerce images used for every product, at every retailer, in every country in the world.\nThe concept, known as \u2018mobile ready hero images\u2019, was designed to make shopping for grocery products faster, by making it easier to quickly spot key information about a product, such as size, type or flavour.\nFor example, searching for \u2018soap\u2019 on Amazon or other retail websites will bring up hundreds of images, and most customers will scroll quickly through the list on their phone in order to find the particular item they want. However, based on product images alone, it can be difficult to quickly spot the differences between items: whether an item contains one, three or ten individual bars of soap, for instance.\nTo date, mobile ready hero images have been adopted by over 80 retailers in more than 40 countries. India \u2013 where 65% of all online shopping transactions take place on mobiles \u2013 has been one of the fastest countries to adopt these images.\nIn order to meet retailers\u2019 demands for consistent product images across all brands, Unilever commissioned Cambridge to develop a website for hero image guidelines, with freely available templates to help brands create improved product images.\nTo date, some brands have created mobile ready hero images using the Cambridge templates, while others have developed hero images in a different way. Some retailers have chosen to accept all kinds of hero images, while others will only accept some kinds of hero images, resulting in an inconsistent experience for consumers.\nGS1, a global non-profit organisation which sets standards for consumer goods, has recently established a working group to focus on mobile ready hero images.\n\u201cInclusive design can help improve the visual clarity of hero images, making them more accessible to a wider range of consumers,\u201d said Waller. \u201cIn particular, our SEE-IT method can estimate the proportion of the population who would be unable to discern the important information from e-commerce images. We have joined the GS1 working group in an advisory capacity, and we are looking forward to contributing our expertise to help inform the critical decisions."
"An incredible breakfront bookcase, made of mahogany in the Georgian style, with incorporated desk. Made circa 1810, this stunning English piece has three glass-front upper cabinets set above a cased plinth base. The upper cabinets have all of their original wavy glass panes, the topmost shaped with Gothic arches. The doors all lock before interiors that are equipped with three adjustable shelves. The interior has been painted a brick red color that makes its contents stand out. The base has a fold-down desk that extends with a clearance of 28 1/4. This space is equipped with seven smaller drawers and four pigeon holes. Three cabinets below each contain fixed shelves, with two large drawers flanking the desk. This commanding piece would make a study or office, but could be used in any large room.\nStunning 18th century English fruitwood breakfront bookcase, 1780. Dimensions: 85.5 inches (217 cms) wide 14.5 inches (37 cms) deep 83 inches (211 cms) high."
