"Chemical spraying for weed and pest control is generally permitted without needing resource consent.\nThe sprays and any drift should be limited to your property to avoid any e\ufb00ects on neighbours or non-target areas.\nSpraying for weed and pest control is generally permitted without a resource consent provided you comply with the following requirements.\nNeighbours must be noti\ufb01ed unless they agree not to be or if the agrichemicals are applied with hand operated and manually pressurised spray equipment. Noti\ufb01cation should include the areas to be sprayed, the dates and times of spraying and the measures that will be used to prevent or minimise spray drift. Different noti\ufb01cation requirements apply if spraying is done from the ground or from the air (see the Regional Air Quality Plan or contact the Council\u2019s consents staff).\nAvoid environmental problems by following the guidelines in our Regional Air Quality Plan and Regional Freshwater Plan. Note the Guidelines in the Freshwater Plan on the use of aquatic herbicides.\nNotify the Council as soon as you can if there is an accidental or unintended discharge of agrichemicals.\nKeep good records of your agrichemical use. This should include the name of the user, the agrichemical equipment and methods of use, the volume and concentration of the agrichemical used, the locality, area and date of application, and the location and nature of any sensitive areas. Your records should also include weather conditions at the time of application, including wind speed and direction and any abnormal situation or incident. You will need to supply these details to the Council on request.\nYou must meet all of these requirements. Otherwise, you will need a resource consent.\nFull details of the requirements can be found in the Regional Air Quality Plan and Regional Freshwater Plan.\nApplying fertiliser to land is generally permitted without the need for a resource consent.\nThe fertiliser does not spread into any water (including streams, lakes, drains or wetlands). Aerial operators should take particular care to avoid waterways.\nThe fertiliser has been approved by the Ministry for Primary Industries.\nThere is minimal chance of the fertiliser drifting off your property or on to non-target areas \u2013 consider the wind direction.\nDust and odour is minimized.\nPungent fertilisers such as chicken litter are not spread on hot, still afternoons or are mixed into soil to reduce odour.\nIt is good practice to prepare a nutrient budget and management plan to provide a total farm system for managing nutrients. This will bene\ufb01t your bottom-line pro\ufb01tability as well as the environment."
"The Washington State Department of Veterans Affairs began construction on the State Veterans Cemetery at Medical Lake in 2009. The $8.8 million project was a collaborative partnership between the Washington State Department of Veterans Affairs and the VA under the Federal VA Cemetery Construction Grant Program. This initial Federal Grant paid 100% of the design and construction costs, and provided for the initial purchase of equipment. The State of Washington was responsible for the initial purchase of the land and all future operating costs. The cemetery recently received a $2.4 million expansion grant for additional capital improvements as part of an ongoing facility master plan. This grant provided for additional interment capacity, improved facility security and addressed additional infrastructure needs.\nFunds from the purchase of Armed Forces License Plates contribute to operation and maintenance expenses of the State Veterans Cemetery. Veterans, active duty, guard, reserve and their family members are eligible to purchase a plate for multiple vehicles at any vehicle licensing office. Fees may be used as tax deductible donations.\nThere are many opportunities for individuals to volunteer their time or resources at the State Veterans Cemetery."
"sorry for the long explanation, but I wanted to make things clear.\nI am a LAMS developer. Till now, we were using JBoss Cache 1.4.2 on JBoss 4.0.2. It was deployed as a MBean and was made available by JMX as \"jboss.cache:service=TreeCache\".\nThe new idea was to use the latest JBoss Cache 3.1.0.\nand we only have one, local instance of Cache.\nThe suggested way of deploying Cache is to use \"-jboss-beans.xml\"\nFor our needs, \"lams-cache-jboss-beans.xml\" was written.\nBut there is problem with Hibernate 3.3 integration. I want to use SharedJBossCacheRegionFactory. It looks for the Cache config file (treecache.xml) and builds its own instance.\nBut in LAMS we need access to cache from withing the code too. We have a special website for Admins where they can lookup and manipulate the cache.\nSo we need a separately deployed Cache instance, like the way we did before.\nCacheFactory and we have only the interface, which can not be exposed.\nThen, there is CacheJmxWrapper class. It wraps the Cache and allows JMX exposition.\nwe have it only in JMX. So we need to expose it to JNDI.\nStandard <jndi-name> tag does not work - parser will not read it.\nIt gets the cache from JMX and exposes it to JNDI.\nCache itself! For our Admin code it is OK, but for Hibernate it's not. It would not accept the exposed bean.\nThe question is: is there a way to expose the Cache itself to JNDI, to make Hibernate accept it and also get it for Admin?\nCurrently SharedJBossCacheRegionFactory is used and a local instance of Cache is created. It is not exposed to JMX, so we do not have access to it from within the code.\nuntil a way to utilise lams-cache-jboss-beans.xml is found.\nNo answer ever came, so I reply to myself.\nFor upcoming LAMS release, we are using pretty much the same version of JBoss as 3 years ago, JBoss 5.1.0.\nThis means that no new tools are available in JBoss to solve cache access problem.\nAfter extensive search, no solution to expose same Cache instance both to Hibernate and CacheManager using JMX/JNDI was found.\nThis may be possible after upgrade to newer major JBoss version, but for LAMS 2.4 another solution was needed.\nCacheManager gets now Hibernate SessionFactory injected by Spring. This way it can in limited manner manipulate 2nd level cache.\nVery frustrating, but this was the best we could do with given tools.\nI've just made a comment in that link, but essentially, it should be Hibernate who, independent of the cache provider, should provide such API to clear all caches in a session factory. I've opened a dev discussion in the Hibernate mailing list.\nBtw, there's new API in Hibernate that allows evicting all entity, collection and query regions, see http://java.dzone.com/tips/clearing-hibernate-second-leve where Steve Ebersole, Hibernate lead, has commented further.\nAt the moment we are using Hibernate 3.2 and the new methods are available in 3.5. We are not planning an upgrade for our upcoming product release, so we'll just stick to the existing solution.\nNevertheless, we might just use this information in the future."
"While technically summer doesn\u2019t start until June 21st, Memorial Day tends to be the unofficial start of the season. With this in mind, the outdoor Summer Movie series in historic Ellicott City has begun, presented by/at The Wine Bin.\nOn Saturday nights from June through September, they close their parking lot an hour before show time, so that viewers can set up chairs or blankets and enjoy any picnic items they\u2019ve brought. Additionally, the Wine Bin offers a wine tasting before each movie and even offers coolers of ice to chill the drinks you purchase from them. You can even purchase popcorn and bottled water, with proceeds benefiting Voices for Children of Howard County.\nMovies through August begin at 9:00pm and then at 8:00pm in September. The selections range from newer movies all the way back to \u201850s classics, with this weekend offering the \u201880s hit Footloose.\nBe sure to check out the summer schedule below, then get out and enjoy these great, free events!"
"My family owns an automotive machine shop where I work. We have done several race motors over the last few years, but we do mostly non-racing work. We have started doing more race engines under the name Maniac Racing Engines. Our engines have several wins in different classes including street stock, modified street, open wheel modified and limited late model.\nI grew up around the race track. My dad raced stock cars and modifieds as long as I can remember. When he finally won the track championship in an IMCA modified, he decided it was time for him to quit racing and let me race full time. We went cart racing, competing in several states across the midsouth. After several successful years in carts, I decided to go car racing. After a decent run my first night out, I drew a front row outside starting position for the second night. On the start of the race I got into the corner too high and left the track airborne and flipped several times, destroying my car. While I was in the process of rebuilding my car, I had the opportunity to team up with a friend and move to the Limited Late Model division. We ran about 10 races in it last season with one win and became a consistent top 5 finisher. So far this season we have won 2 races at 2 different tracks and are 7th in points only 23 behind the leader at Thunderhill Raceway in Summertown,TN.\nMy current ride is a 2012 Pierce by Putnam Chassis powered by a Maniac Racing Engine Limited Late Model."
