"The safest and most effective Pycnogenol dosage has not yet been clearly established. Because Pycnogenol is a dietary supplement, it does not have to go through dose-range studies (as medications do) to determine the safest and most effective dose. However, the studies that have been done on Pycnogenol used doses ranging between 25 mg and 360 mg each day.\nWhat Is the Recommended Dosage of Pycnogenol?\nA recommended Pycnogenol\u00ae dosage has not yet been clearly established. Unlike medications, for which the standard doses have been well established in carefully designed studies, there is less information available for determining the best dose for most herbal supplements, including Pycnogenol.\nIs There a Reasonable Pycnogenol Dose?\nWith both prescription and non-prescription medications, researchers establish the most effective and safest doses in special studies, known as dose-range studies. These studies are done early in the development of medications, long before they are ever approved. However, because herbal supplements do not require approval by the U.S. Food and Drug Administration (FDA), dose-range studies are rarely performed. Without such studies, only vague \"trial and error\" information is available.\nEven though Pycnogenol has undergone several studies, it is not known if the doses used in these studies are the safest and most effective. Read the label of your particular Pycnogenol product carefully and consult with your healthcare provider in order to find a reasonable dose for your situation. In general, studies used Pycnogenol doses ranging from 25 mg to 360 mg per day. Some studies split the dose into two or three smaller doses per day, while other studies used a once-daily dose.\nBecause the manufacturing of supplements such Pycnogenol is not closely regulated, it is important to choose a trustworthy manufacturer of your supplements. Your pharmacist is a good source of information regarding which supplement manufacturers are more reputable than others. Even though Pycnogenol is a patented extract that seems to contain a high-quality active ingredient, the supplements themselves, which are made by several different companies, may vary in quality.\nIt is probably best to take Pycnogenol with meals or just after meals, as this may decrease or eliminate stomach upset.\nIf you have any chronic health problems or take any prescription medications, you should check with your healthcare provider before taking Pycnogenol.\nIf you are unsure about anything related to your dosage or Pycnogenol dosing in general, please talk with your healthcare provider.\nJellin JM, editor. Pharmacist's Letter/Prescriber's Letter Natural Medicines Comprehensive Database Web site. Available at: http://naturaldatabase.com/. Accessed January 23, 2008.\nPycnogenol Web site. Available at: http://www.pycnogenol.com. Accessed January 23, 2008."
"Jennifer Burd\u2019s newest poetry collection explores the many ways we deal with loss.\nJennifer Burd\u2019s second full-length collection, Day\u2019s Late Blue, was released by Cherry Grove Collections last year.\nits scarves upon the river.\nInterested in both loss and growth, Burd shows us how life goes on after losing a loved one. We learn that things come and go, but we can always grow from what we lose by cherishing the environment that surrounds us.\nEven though the speaker is drained of energy, she admires nature, watching the animals that work hard for their own survival and how they somehow know exactly what they need to do. The imagery in these first two stanzas immediately sets up the poem\u2019s tone and relates back to the theme of loss. The enjambment here also leaves the reader hanging from line to line. This is seen in lines four and five: \u201cwatching the birds / in their quick-numbered / moves.\u201d Burd leaves a phrase on the edge for the reader\u2019s contemplation while also keeping them focused on the poem.\nNot only is it traumatic to suffer a loved one\u2019s death, but these two stanzas convey a feeling that it is even worse when it is slow and painful. Throughout the poem, the speaker\u2019s mother\u2019s memory worsens. Sometimes it is not just us that lose them, but that they slowly lose us, too.\nand still changing, seeing this autumn through.\nAssistant poetry editor Taylor Worsham is a senior in the Creative Writing program at Lake Superior State University. Her work has appeared in Snowdrifts; last spring, she was the 2018 recipient of the Stellanova Osborn Poetry Scholarship. Taylor currently resides in Sault Sainte Marie, Michigan."
"\u0418\u0437 \u044f\u0432\u043d\u044b\u0445 \u043d\u0435\u0434\u043e\u0441\u0442\u0430\u0442\u043a\u043e\u0432 \u0442\u043e, \u0447\u0442\u043e \u043e\u043d\u0438 \u043f\u0440\u043e\u0434\u0430\u044e\u0442 20% \u043e\u0442 \u0432\u0441\u0435\u0445 \u0442\u043e\u043a\u0435\u043d\u043e\u0432 \u0437\u0430 45\u043c\u043b\u043d.\n\u0422\u0430\u043a\u0436\u0435 \u043c\u043e\u0436\u043d\u043e \u0437\u0430\u0439\u0442\u0438 \u0442\u043e\u043b\u044c\u043a\u043e \u0432 \u0444\u043e\u0440\u043c\u0430\u0442\u0435 \u043f\u0440\u0435\u0441\u0435\u0439\u043b\u0430, \u043e\u0442 50 \u044d\u0444\u0438\u0440\u043e\u0432 \u043c\u0438\u043d\u0438\u043c\u0443\u043c, \u043e\u0431\u044b\u0447\u043d\u043e\u0435 ICO \u043e\u0442\u043c\u0435\u043d\u0438\u043b\u0438.\n\u042f \u0434\u0443\u043c\u0430\u044e \u043a\u0443\u043f\u0438\u0442\u044c \u043d\u0430 100 \u044d\u0444\u0438\u0440\u0430. \u043e\u0442\u043f\u0440\u0430\u0432\u0438\u043b \u0438\u043c \u043f\u0438\u0441\u044c\u043c\u043e - \u0436\u0434\u0443 \u043e\u0442\u0432\u0435\u0442\u0430.\n40% (development and ecosystem) : some of it will be reserved for long term (>2 years) projects (and will be completely frozen until that point). The rest will be used to facilitate the creation, prosperity and expansion of the ecosystem, through subsidizing of predictions. Namely - entrepreneurs, academic researchers and early users will be given tokens that can be used for predictions only. This will not devalue the token, but on the contrary - will enable the fast creation of businesses around EDR (=more demand) and support information (more accurate predictions ==> more demand).\n20% (partnerships) : will be discussed on a case by case basis, The goals of this is to enable strategic partnerships through the access to large-scale predictions at a low cost ==> as an investment for the creation of sustainable business at a later stage. Therefore - liquidating the tokens is not the idea here, and we would discuss appropriate mechanisms with our partners.\nFor example, EDRs can be allocated as proxy for other services in the crypto sphere (e.g. a Bancor Relay). In this cases, the EDRs are frozen, and act as an enabler of the overlaying services, with zero liquidation.\n\u0421\u043e\u0431\u0438\u0440\u0430\u044e\u0442 45 \u043c\u0438\u043b\u043b\u0438\u043e\u043d\u043e\u0432- \u044d\u0442\u043e \u0434\u043e\u0444\u0438\u0433\u0430, \u0442\u0432\u0438\u0442\u0442\u0435\u0440 - 2\u043a \u043f\u043e\u0434\u043f\u0438\u0441\u0447\u0438\u043a\u043e\u0432, \u043c\u0438\u043d. \u0443\u0447\u0430\u0441\u0442\u0438\u0435 50 ETH. \u041d\u0435, \u043d\u0430\u0445\u0440\u0435\u043d.\n\u042f \u0434\u0435\u043d\u044c\u0433\u0438 \u043d\u0435 \u0431\u0443\u0434\u0443\u0442 \u0441\u043e\u0431\u0438\u0440\u0430\u0442\u044c \u0432\u043e\u043e\u0431\u0449\u0435, \u0435\u0441\u043b\u0438 \u0437\u0430\u0439\u0434\u0443 - \u044f \u043e\u0441\u0442\u0430\u0432\u043b\u044e \u044d\u0444\u0438\u0440 \u043a\u043e\u0448\u0435\u043b\u0435\u043a, \u043a\u0443\u0434\u0430 \u043f\u0440\u043e\u0441\u0442\u043e \u043f\u0435\u0440\u0435\u0432\u0435\u0434\u0435\u0442\u0435, \u0430 \u043f\u043e\u0442\u043e\u043c \u0440\u0430\u0441\u043a\u0438\u043d\u0443 \u043d\u0430\u0437\u0430\u0434 \u043d\u0430 \u044d\u0442\u0438 \u043a\u043e\u0448\u0435\u043b\u044c\u043a\u0438 \u0442\u043e\u043a\u0435\u043d\u044b, \u043a\u043e\u0433\u0434\u0430 \u043f\u0440\u0438\u0434\u0443\u0442 \u0438 \u0432\u0441\u0435.\n\u042f \u043f\u0440\u0438\u043d\u044f\u043b \u0440\u0435\u0448\u0435\u043d\u0438\u0435 \u043d\u0435 \u0443\u0447\u0430\u0441\u0442\u0432\u043e\u0432\u0430\u0442\u044c.\n\u041d\u0443 \u0431\u043e\u043d\u0443\u0441 \u0442\u0430\u043c \u043e\u0442 500-1000eth \u043a\u043e\u0433\u0434\u0430, \u043f\u0443\u043b\u044b \u0442\u043e \u043a\u0443\u043f\u044f\u0442 \u043a\u0440\u0443\u043f\u043d\u044b\u0435, \u043f\u0440\u043e\u0441\u0442\u043e \u043d\u0435 \u0432\u0438\u0436\u0443 \u0441\u043c\u044b\u0441\u043b\u0430 \u0438\u043c\u0435\u043d\u043d\u043e \u0432 \u0442\u0435\u043a\u0443\u0449\u0435\u0439 \u0441\u0438\u0442\u0443\u0430\u0446\u0438\u0438."
"If you work construction, accidents are more likely to happen than if you worked an office job (though of course, accidents happen there too).\nJob sites are fraught with opportunities to trip and fall. Uneven, slippery surfaces are the most likely culprit, but sometimes the very devices that are there for your safety could be at fault, such as improperly constructed scaffolding, faulty or worn safety harnesses, ladders that aren\u2019t securely anchored, etc.\nSuch \u201cmoving objects\u201d could include a tool dropped from an overhead platform or scaffold, a stray nail or staple ejected from a pneumatic tool, collapsed masonry, or even a vehicle whose driver didn\u2019t properly set an emergency brake or take proper care in driving through a work area.\nPower lines are always one of the biggest concerns when seeking to prevent construction accidents, because this type of hazard can be hanging overhead or hiding underground. In addition, tools that require electricity must be properly grounded, or anyone in the immediate vicinity is at risk.\nThese types of construction accidents are the definition of being in the wrong place at the wrong time. This category includes getting caught under unsecured heavy machinery, unstable piles of materials such as gravel, and deep trench cave-ins.\nConstruction accidents often result in serious injuries such as concussion, limbs that require amputation, blindness, deafness, paralysis, permanent scarring, and death. Survivors often find that their ability to make a living in the future is impacted. That\u2019s why it\u2019s important to keep all your records in order and consult an attorney as soon as possible after construction accidents.\nConsult with the lawyers at Pat Maloney. We\u2019ll help you make the best comeback possible."
"Hosted by Jon Kulp on 2016-06-16 is flagged as Clean and is released under a CC-BY-SA license.\nIn this episode I show how to start adding more commands, how to use the language updater script, and how to start doing some basic desktop navigation. I'll show you how to open and quit applications, and how to switch from one application to another using your voice.\nFor information about installing blather for the first time, as well as the startup script that I use, please refer to episode 0 of this series, which has examples and links for this stuff.\nCommands are configured in a \"key: value\" pair, where the key is what you wish to say, and the value is the command that will be executed when you say it. We will start out with some very basic ones, but these can be as elaborate as your imagination and scripting skills will allow. You can execute built-in system commands, or you can write your own scripts that will be executed upon the voice command.\nGO TO CHROMIUM: wmctrl -a \"google chrome\"\nQUIT CHROMIUM: wmctrl -c \"google chrome\"\nThe first command launches Chromium, the second one will switch focus to Chromium when you are currently in another program, and the third one closes Chromium. This makes use of the command line tool wmctrl, which is a very handy window management tool. The wmctrl -a command chooses which window to put focus on (or close) based on the window title, which in the commands above is given in quotation marks. There are many options to how wmctrl can find windows and take actions, but for now we will just use this basic option.\nOnce you have one command set of this kind working as you like, it's very easy to set up additional command sets for all of the desktop applications you use most often.\nGO TO clementine: rid=$(pgrep clementine -u $(whoami) |head -n 1) && rwinname=$(wmctrl -lp |grep $rid |sed -e \"s/.*$rid * //\" | sed -e \"s/$(hostname) //\") && wmctrl -a \"$rwinname\"\nOpening the music player is easy. Switching to it is something else. To make this work I first find the process ID of the Clementine music player, and then I use the wmctrl list command to list all of the windows that are open and I grep for the process ID that I found in the first part. Then I extract the window name from that command's output and use the result inside quotation marks in the very last command to change Focus to that window. Whew!\nThis makes use of the wonderful xdotool package to execute a virtual keystroke. Magic!\nWould this work for headless computers. I mean opening and closing minetest server running on a single board computer.\nYes, I think so. I'm pretty sure that Jezra uses this on a headless computer in his house. You might go back and listen to my interview with Jezra from episode 1284 (http://hackerpublicradio.org/eps.php?id=1284) and see what he says about it."
