"Jeremy Bicha wrote up an unknown Ubuntu feature: \u201cprinting\u201d direct to a Google Drive PDF. I rather wanted this, but I don\u2019t run the Gnome desktop, so I thought I might be out of luck. But no! It works fine on my Ubuntu MATE desktop too. A couple of extra tweaks are required, though. This is unfortunately a bit technical, but it should only need setting up once.\nYou need the Gnome Control Centre and Gnome Online Accounts installed, if you don\u2019t have them already, as well as the Google Cloud Print extension that Jeremy mentions. From a terminal, run sudo apt install gnome-control-center gnome-online-accounts cpdb-backend-gcp.\nThen\u2026 it all works. From now on, when you go to print something, the print dialogue will, after a couple of seconds, show a new entry: \u201cSave to Google Drive\u201d. Choose that, and your document will \u201cprint\u201d to a PDF stored in Google Drive. Easy peasy. Nice one Jeremy for the write-up. It\u2019d be neat if Ubuntu MATE could integrate this a little more tightly."
"As the month of January draws to a close, complete with overcast skies and dreary weather, I found myself wanting a bit of happiness and color. I set out to create a happy and welcoming entry for Valentine\u2019s Day. Let My Love Open The Door Valentine Wreath.\nI first looked to see what I had on hand: a bit of open weave burlap I bought on clearance last fall, Christmas clearance red ribbon and a wire wreath form. It was a start.\nI thought maybe threading the ribbon through the burlap would be cute. (Holy time waster!) It wasn\u2019t quite right. So I tried adding some other red and white ribbon. I only got half way through when I decided it was an epic fail.\nSo I pulled everything apart. Sometimes creativity happens that way. But you can\u2019t give up. You might need a little Pete Townsend to bring back the spark.\nBy adding some red burlap ~ suddenly ~ everything worked!\nIn the meantime, I had gotten down the small tote of Valentine d\u00e9cor and found some old wooden hearts I have had for years.\nThey came complete with a key and begged to be tied on with a big burlap bow.\nOh, the front entry was singing now!\nI love your Valentine's Day Wreath!! You pulled it all together!\nThis is gorgeous Laura! I love love love love that burlap and the hearts!!\nBeautiful! And thanks for the encouragement about epic fails, I usually give up \u2026 but now I know you can just keep trying til you nail it!\nSimply stunning pretty lady! You are someone who exudes LOVE, so I thank God for putting you in my path as a friend.\nLove all the burlap wreaths\u2026so cute and clever! Thanx for sharing.\nLove it, Laura! The red burlap does it for me. : ) The wooden hearts make for a great finishing touch, too.\nLaura, your wreath is lovely! It's comforting to know another lady that sings on her blog! I was singing \u201cJourney\u201d while writing my post at 4:15 this morning.\nGorgeous wreath! Love how it all came together.\nHi Laura! Oh, I love your wreath! I've never worked with anything burlap! You're changing my tiny mind.\nOh it is so pretty! LOVE the red burlap!\nYour wreath is amazing . . . SO PRETTY!!! You are so very talented. Love the red burlap.\nWOW! I don't always go for the super crafty stuff, but I really, really want to give this a try!!!\nBeautiful Laura \u2013 I love the red burlap.\nI had better not click the video link because I will have that song stuck in my head all night. I'm like that with songs with repetitive lyrics.\nI love the wreath! I need one for my front door that says Valentines Day before it's too late.\nI love your wreath! The contrast of the red and soft natural burlap plus the different weaves\u2026what a great idea! And adding a key, oh be still my heart! lol It's soooo pretty.\nOh Laura I am so excited about your new venture and can't wait. Your Valentine Wreath has once again brightened my day.\nPlease keep me in your prayers for Monday and thank you so much for your help!\nYour Valentine's Wreath is just the cure for winter dulldrums. Love the pop of bold red paired with the netted burlap. The heart trio complete with locks and key are the perfect finishing touch!\nsuper adorable Laura!!!! But knowing you, you can come up with anything beautiful with just about anything in your craft box! Love it!\nOh this Valentine's wreath is gorgeous! So lovely \u2013 I have to pin this!\nHi Laura \u2013 Thanks for visiting and leaving such a nice comment about my 'Ruffled Pillow Tutorial'. Your wreath turned out so pretty. The red burlap was the perfect addition.\nYou make gorgeous wreaths! I love the hearts, the keys, the red burlap, and the open weave burlap\u2026 I guess I love everything about this wreath! I'm pinning it to my Valentine's board, and I'm gonna have to follow you!\nSuch a welcoming wreath and it definitely will brighten a dreary day!! Love the poems and the sentiment \u2013 Happy Valentine's Day!\nCupid for sure, will be knocking on your door! Beautiful.."
"MGH. The marketing agency that Darwin built.\nMGH began more than 20 years ago by disposing of the \"rules\" that had defined advertising agencies for so long. The first thing to go was ego. From Day 1, we recognized that none of us is as smart as all of us. That an idea becomes better when it is shared, and challenged, and built upon.\nOur philosophy? Great ideas come together.\nThey may start in the creative department. Or account service. Or social media marketing. Or public relations. Or web development. Or media buying and planning. Or brand strategy. They may come from one of our clients. But our ideas truly blossom when people from all of these disciplines gather in a room to talk and laugh and listen and debate. And sometimes eat pizza.\nToday, as the marketing world evolves, we remain true to the words of the father of evolution, Charles Darwin. \"In the long history of humankind,\" he once wrote, \"those who learned to collaborate and improvise most effectively have prevailed.\"\nLook at our current clients. Don\u2019t you want to be one of them? Imagine how exciting it would feel to see your logo along with all these others. Well, it would be exciting for us, anyway.\nMeet our staff. They\u2019re smart, really experienced, and perhaps best of all: They\u2019re not jerks. Seriously, these are nice people.\nThe kind you\u2019d have a beer with. If they paid.\nThe queen of social media ad campaigns. And the chief of the \"Quiet Police\" at MGH, for those times when things get noisy.\nDesigns for the digital world. From a cube overlooking the real world.\nOnce memorized Pi to 80+ digits. So she\u2019s pretty good at counting all the media impressions our clients receive.\nPuts clients on the air, online and in print. Puts snacks in her desk for hungry co-workers.\nA rock star on our web team, who also plays guitar and piano.\nInspires staff to think bigger, so our clients grow bigger every day. Also pays for bagels every Friday.\nShe gets the expense reports. We must be very nice to her.\nLoves to go camping. When she's not creating campaigns.\nThe first person you see when you walk into MGH. The last person you see after hiring us and heading to the elevator.\nMedia is in his blood. Need proof? His twin brother also works for us.\nCan write virtually anything with ease, style and grace. Except perhaps this caption.\nKnows where the value is hidden, and goes after it. Also really enjoys his 90-minute commute from D.C.\nHits a mean tennis ball. And is great at pitching the news media, too.\nDesigns the cool stuff on Facebook you can't scroll by. And never sits down: She's the first MGHer to use a standing desk.\nSmart, organized, and relentless in helping clients achieve their goals. Plus, she has nice shoes.\nWhen she\u2019s not at work analyzing digital media, you\u2019ll find her at the movies analyzing plots.\nExpert at promoting our clients on a national stage. While sitting modestly back stage.\nUses an unusual language for a web developer: English. Perhaps that\u2019s why he\u2019s in demand on the web lecture circuit.\nA self-described television addict. But only because she needs to stay current for her clients. That\u2019s what she says, anyway.\nInspires great creative in others by producing great creative himself. Also has the best hair in the agency.\nHelps keep the human resources of MGH feeling happy and sane. No small feat.\nBelieves every dollar counts when it comes to media. He's even got \"cash\" in his name.\nLooks like a guy who knows what he\u2019s doing. Bonus: He actually does.\nA calm, cool PR professional. Who also giggles a lot.\nLoves tacos, donuts, and social media. Not necessarily in that order.\nShe\u2019s obsessed with dogs. And making her clients happy. But mostly dogs.\nAccounting star by day. Shining star by night, often volunteering her time to many a good cause.\nManages social media accounts, from content to client communication. Resident cat lady.\nDrives her kids to soccer, basketball, baseball, lacrosse and golf. And in her spare time, works at MGH.\nDrives employees to be their best. Drives employees to lunch now and then.\nLoves to cook and make things from scratch. Like media plans, for instance.\nKnows how to get it printed, folded, built or mailed. Noted workaholic, despite our best attempts to find him a hobby.\nSets a mighty high bar for his creative teams. Then often meets them at that bar after work.\nComfortable traversing rough waters in a kayak. And in a marketing agency.\nMakes websites happen through a combination of organization, attention to detail, and magic. Mostly magic.\nExecutes PR for public safety, tech and healthcare clients. Executes co-workers who insist on microwaving seafood at work.\nHandles traditional media buying. Without ever standing on tradition.\nResponsible for media planning and buying. And making co-workers fat with her homemade cookies.\nRemains calm and collected during a PR crisis. Possibly less so while traveling with her two young children.\nStirs up publicity for her PR clients. Stirs up a mean pot of homemade soup.\nManages the world of social media. Travels the world in her spare time.\nKeeps the wheels of account service moving in the right direction. Has the coolest name in the agency.\nWears many hats at MGH. All of them party hats.\nShe once wrote for an Italian newspaper. And has now mastered the language of social media.\nWorks closely with MGH clients. Having once been a client of MGH.\nManages her clients with confidence and kindness. Which also seems to describe her personality.\nHas a strategy for everything. But hopefully not an exit strategy.\nNick-named Wavey Maevey. Graphic Designer by day - travel designer by night.\nMakes organized people look disorganized. But always with a smile.\nA mighty strong designer. And, as a Crossfit junkie, a mighty strong person, period.\nBorn in Denmark. Works remotely from Texas. But no matter where she lives, we\u2019re happy to say she\u2019s with us.\nSpent 14 years on the softball field. Now at home in the social media field.\nMakes his social media accounts sing. Also likes to sing. Way too much.\nShe grew up in Texas. So naturally she likes big results from her social media campaigns.\nIntimately familiar with dozens of media markets around the country. Not to mention Ocean City, Maryland, her second home.\nKnows how to buy, where to buy and who to buy. Also buys a lot of stuff on Amazon.\nKeeps our computer systems running smoothly. And safe from Russian advertising hackers.\nBorn for social media. And our youngest employee, born in XXXX.\nA successful advertising copywriter. A (so far) unsuccessful New Yorker humorist.\nNever met a social media trend he didn\u2019t capitalize on, blog about, or exploit for our clients\u2019 benefit. Plus, he once tattooed the Twitter logo on his leg.\nKnown for her online shopping prowess. And her on-message PR skills.\nAn expert on SEM. Thanks in part to her MBA.\nPays the bills, pays the staff, pays attention to the bottom line. Doesn\u2019t pay for company jets, sadly.\nShe doesn\u2019t have an eye for detail. She\u2019s got two.\nSkilled at online shopping. And analyzing online shoppers.\nLoves a good spin class. Which explains why she's so adept at PR.\nLet\u2019s make beautiful marketing together. Call us."
