"Perhaps the pertinent question to ask before we start this study is, \u2018Do you believe you are a Christian? Secondly, \u201cWhat makes you believe you are a Christian? There has been an error among Christians for ages now that they are Christians because they belong to a Christian denomination or are born into a Christian family. However nothing can be farther from the truth than this. A Christian is a new creature. If really you are a Christian when did you become a new creature? You will get more from this book.\nTEXT: 2Co 5:17. Therefore if any man be in Christ, he is a new creature: old things are passed away; behold, all things are become new.\nPerhaps the pertinent question to ask before we start this study is, \u2018Do you believe you are a Christian? Secondly, \"What makes you believe you are a Christian? There has been an error among Christians for ages now that they are Christians because they belong to a Christian denomination or are born into a Christian family. However nothing can be farther from the truth than this.\nThe scripture above says something that is very interesting \u2013 A Christian is a new creature. If really you are a Christian when did you become a new creature?\nThere is yet another scripture.\nJohn 3:3. Jesus answered and said unto him, Verily, verily, I say unto thee, Except a man be born again, he cannot see the kingdom of God.\nThe kingdom of God belongs to His children, therefore if the Word of God says that those who are not born again cannot see the kingdom of God it means that this group of people do not belong to God. They are not his children.\nThere is a third scripture that is important here.\nJohn 1:12-13. But as many as received him, to them gave he power to become the sons of God, even to them that believe on his name: Which were born, not of blood, nor of the will of the flesh, nor of the will of man, but of God.\nWho are the people that God gave the power to become His Children? It is those that received him. And what does it mean to receive Him? Have you received him? To receive and him is to admit and believe that he is the Lord of your life. To receive a head of state or a governor, you must first recognize his office that he occupies and honor and treat him as such. To receive Christ in your life you must give him his rightful place as your Lord. That is where the proper relationship starts.\nReceiving Christ is a spiritual process. It happens inside, in the spirit of the person concerned. This means that if a call is made and someone comes out to answer the call, without this internal change he/she is still not a child of God."
"CITRACKS, a subsidiary of C&I Leasing and its technical partner, Galooli, have unveiled cost-saving technology solutions on fleet and power management.\nDuring a telematics conference to unveil the solutions in Lagos, the organizers said that the technology solutions were capable of saving operational cost of organizations for as much as 30 percent.\nAside from tracking solutions, the telematics conference also highlighted how telecommunications companies as well as oil and gas companies, among others, could manage fuel consumption and maintain their generators using the best of technology solutions that saves cost.\nThe Managing Director/ Chief Executive Officer, C&I Leasing, Mr. Andrew Otikeodibi, said the company introduced Citracks as one of its business units to address the imbalance in fleet and power businesses in the county.\nHe said the telematics conference was put together to highlight technology solutions that would help organizations and individuals to manage their fleet of vehicles and generators in the most cost-effective manner that would save them as high as 30 percent in their operational cost.\nHe said that Citracks had gone beyond the transportation business of vehicle tracking and fleet management, to a full technology business company using telematics to provide technology solutions for organizations.\nThe Managing Director, Galooli, Mr. Nissim Moshe, described the partnership between Citracks and Galooli as one that had used technology and telematics to change the face of business in Nigeria since Galooli entered into the market in 2016.\nAccording to him, the app is available on Google Play Store for download on any Android device and comes with great ease of use.\n\u201cOur app solution monitors mobile assets as well as physical stationery assets, and helps customers to know how those assets are being utilized and managed.\n\u201cFor vehicles, we are looking at how the vehicle is utilized and operated and what is the fuel consumption of the vehicle and how the driver is managing the vehicle and its fuel consumption,\u201d Fraser said.\nWould like to have a look at a demo on your vehicle trucking system."
"SANTA MONICA, CA \u2014 Company 3 had added Jeremy Sawyer (Hulu's The First, Showtime\u2019s I\u2019m Dying up Here) to its roster of colorists to accommodate the studio\u2019s rapidly expanding slate of TV projects. Company 3 has more than 20 television series currently undergoing post at the facility, including Lost in Space (Netflix), Insecure (HBO) and Jack Ryan (Amazon).\nSawyer has distinguished himself on a number of major series, including The Walking Dead (AMC) and The Affair (Showtime). He brings a refined sensibility and deeply-entrenched understanding of the TV post workflow.\nThe move is somewhat of a homecoming for Sawyer. As an assistant and then colorist at Company 3, he learned a great deal of his craft from CEO/founder Stefan Sonnenfeld. He returns to the company following a successful tenure at Light Iron and before that, MTI. Prior to his initial stint at Company 3, Sawyer worked at the now defunct Syndicate. He started his career in post production at Finish Post in his native Boston.\n\u201cWe\u2019re very excited to welcome Jeremy back,\" Sonnenfeld says. \u201cHe is an excellent artist and he has a keen understanding of the unique challenges involved in coloring episodic programming. He's a perfect addition to our team, especially as demand for top-notch TV post continues to explode.\"\n\"I'm very excited to be back at Company 3,\" Sawyer adds. \"I'm grateful for the opportunities I've had since I left, but there's really nothing like being part of Company 3's incredible talent roster and having the company's amazing technical powerhouse behind me!\"\nSawyer will continue his work on the third season of the Netflix series, Easy, for which he's colored every episode to date."
"Having gear is an ever-present feature of kayaking as a hobby. You don't have to carry a huge amount of stuff around on your kayak, but having your sunscreen, water, and first aid kit stored in your deck bag within easy reach helps to make the already amazing experience of kayaking that much better.\nDeck bags are very well-suited for certain types of kayaks. Does your long, narrow touring kayak lack storage space? Are you struggling to find waterproof storage areas for your valuables in your sit-on recreational kayak? Then a deck bag is exactly what you need to solve your storage problems!\nKayak balancing is another prime use for a deck bag. If you are packing a lot of gear into your kayak for camping or going on a long expedition, then you might find your kayak becomes very back- or front-heavy, making it harder to paddle. This is usually because the design of the kayak places the majority of the storage at one end of the boat. Using a deck bag helps even out the weight, improving the speed and tracking of your kayak.\nBeing able to easily access your bag without compromising your balance is vital. When you are choosing a deck bag, make sure its zippers are in the right places to ensure you have easy access. Before you head out on the water, try attaching your bag to your kayak and then make sure you can get to all the zippers, pockets, and pouches without needing to maneuver in unusual ways.\nThere are several different designs of deck bag fasteners and it\u2019s best to have an idea of which one you would prefer before you start looking to buy. Bags with waterproof zippers are easier to access in a hurry and a lot less fiddly. The downside is that waterproof zippers damage over time and are expensive to replace.\nRoll top bags do exactly what they say: the waterproof material on the bag extends enough that you can close it at the top, usually with clips or magnetic bands, and then roll it down to provide a waterproof seal. The problem with roll top bags are is that they take a little more effort to open and close, which can get irritating if you are the kind of kayaker that needs access to their gear more often than not.\nThere is a distinct difference between the terms \u201cwaterproof\u201d and \u201cwater-resistant\u201d. Bags that are \u201cwater-resistant\u201d are fine for items that you would prefer to be kept dry, but it\u2019s not the end of the world if they do get wet. For items that must be kept absolutely dry, such as cameras, mobile phone, and radios, you\u2019ll probably want to invest in a bag that identifies itself as entirely \u201cwaterproof\u201d or use a smaller separate waterproof bags inside a larger water-resistant bag to keep them safe.\nIf you go kayaking, it\u2019s almost guaranteed you will roll your kayak at some point. It\u2019s also not a big deal as getting back into your kayak is actually quite easy when you know how. The one thing you don\u2019t want is your deck bag coming free during the roll and floating off with all your gear, or even worse, sinking with it.\nWhen you are looking to buy a deck bag, consider how it will attach to your kayak and how sturdy those attachments are. If the bag has D-rings then it might be worth checking if your kayak has attachment points for them. If the straps are made to go all the way around the kayak, it\u2019s always best to measure beforehand to make sure they will fit.\nBefore you pick out a deck bag you like, think about how many items you will be fitting in it. If it's just for carrying everyday gear then you might not need much more than a 5-liter bag. If you\u2019re heading off camping or going on a long kayaking expedition, you may need something with a little more space, maybe up to a 20-liter bag. If you are planning on buying a larger bag, make sure it fits on the hull of your kayak securely before heading out on the water. A poorly-fitted deck bag can destabilize your kayak and make it hard to access your gear.\nWith a hefty 400-denier nylon shell over a fully waterproof Infinity polyurethane inner bag liner and topped off by a Hydro Lock zip-seal, the Taj M'Haul deck bag is the best way to keep your valuables safe and dry.\nIn addition to its spacious inner picket, the Taj M'Haul has an additional large outer pocket and mesh sleeves for easy access to waterproof items. Buckles on the outside of bag can be cinched up when the pockets are empty to keep the bag nice and streamlined.\nHard wearing nylon hook and loop attachments should keep your fears of your bag going AWOL to a minimum, but if it does end up in the water, don\u2019t panic. The Taj M'Haul is fully buoyant and has reflective tape to make it as visible as possible, even at night.\nOnce you are back on dry land, the Taj M'Haul has a convenient carry strap, making it easy to get your gear to and from the boat. The only slight downside to this bag is that the plastic insert used for keeping the bag open is not quite rigid enough and can deform.\nDespite the comedic name, the Taj M'Haul is an excellent deck bag. It\u2019s tough, fully waterproof, easy-to-use, and has a ton of useful features. The easy open insert could be a little more rigid, but that\u2019s a small complaint on an otherwise fantastic bag, which is why we\u2019ve made it our choice for best deck bag.\nMade of damage-resistant TPU-coated ripstop nylon and coming with corrosion resistant zippers as standard, the Aquatidal deck bag is ready to take the worst your kayaking trips can throw at it.\nThe large and spacious main pocket it water-resistant, though not fully waterproof, so your vulnerable valuables will need to be stored in their own fully waterproof containers. At 25 liters, the Aquatidal deck bag provides plenty of storage and has three separate pockets to help you organize your gear.\nOn the outside of the bag, there are two mesh pockets to store water resistant gear or water bottle, and it has both bungee and steel D-ring attachments to secure it to your boat. To help you access your stuff, the Aquatidal deck bag comes with an internal stiffener board to help it keep its shape and for when you are back on land, a shoulder strap to help you carry your gear around.\nA close contender to the Taj M'Haul, the Aquatidal deck bag loses out because it\u2019s not fully waterproof. Other than that, it is a fantastic bag. The TPU-coated ripstop nylon makes it hugely durable and its multiple pockets, both internal and external, make accessing your gear that much easier.\nIf you\u2019re on a budget and want a deck bag that is lightweight, durable, and you aren\u2019t concerned about what\u2019s inside getting wet, then the Seattle Sports Mesh deck bag is a good pick. We should say upfront that this bag is in no way waterproof or water resistant. It\u2019s made of mesh, so if you need something to store your valuables in then you are better off looking at the Taj M'Haul bag.\nHowever, if you just want a compact deck bag to store durable gear in, then the Seattle Sports Mesh deck bag is a good choice! It weighs just 10.8 oz. and has a single easy-to-open heavy-duty zipper for better access. One of the benefits of it being a mesh bag is that it doubles as a drink cooler: pop your bottles in the bag and lower it over the side to keep it cool in the water. Just make sure you have secured to the hull first!\nIf you want what is a basic mesh bag that holds gear and attaches to your kayak, then the Seattle Sports Mesh deck bag is ideal. Plus, it can keep your drinks cool. If you want any other features at all, then you\u2019ll need to look elsewhere.\nA medium-sized bag with a single large 8-liter compartment, the Attwood deck storage bag combines a durable PVC outer with a nylon inner for additional water resistance. You won\u2019t want to put anything that is extremely vulnerable to water in this bag, and if it does get submerged then its contents will get wet.\nThe Attwood deck storage bag comes with both steel D-ring and snap-cord attachments to easily secure it to your kayak, making sure that it doesn\u2019t move around and is stable when you need to access your gear.\nAnother benefit of the Attwood deck storage bag is its bright color and reflective striping. If you do a lot of kayak fishing at night or in foggy areas, then the Attwood helps to keep you visible.\nThe Attwood deck storage bag is essentially an upgrade on the Seattle Sports Mesh deck bag in terms of durability and water-resistance. Much like the Seattle Sports Mesh deck bag, it is well suited to those looking for a simple, easy-to-use bag. Those looking for more features would be better considering the Aquatidal deck bag or the Taj M'Haul.\nThe Sea to Summit Solution Access deck bag is constructed of 420-denier polyurethane coated ripstop nylon, making its exterior extremely durable. The inner lining is a roll-top water-resistant bag to make sure everything stays nice and dry. The bag keeps its shape through a stiff plastic insert, making it easier to access your gear.\nThe Sea to Summit Solution Access deck bag attaches to your kayak through nylon straps and steel D-rings to keep it nice and secure. At 1.2 pounds, the bag is lightweight and portable. Although the single inner pocket doesn\u2019t provide you with a huge amount of space, it should be noted that the Solution Access deck bag will fit on even the narrowest of touring kayaks.\nThe Sea to Summit Solution Access deck bag is an excellent choice if you are expecting your deck bag to see a lot of wear, or if you have a particularly narrow kayak. It\u2019s highly durable, its inner liner makes it fully waterproof, and although it doesn\u2019t give you a huge amount of storage space, it is compact and lightweight.\nThe Advanced Elements Quickdraw deck bag is constructed in two parts. The exterior is made of hard-wearing and water-resistant PVC covered tarpaulin. The interior is a removable roll-top drybag to help you organize your gear and keep the truly valuable things totally dry. Should you ever feel the need to remove in the inner drybag, you have the option to do so.\nWhile the Advanced Elements Quickdraw deck bag isn\u2019t the largest or most spacious of bags, it is longer and more compact than some of bigger bags on this list, making it ideal for smaller or slimmer kayaks. It does have a water-resistant zipper to help keep some of that water out.\nAttachment to your kayak is taken care of by four steel D-rings and the bag itself has bungee cording on the outside for extra attachments, or to store items that you don't mind getting wet.\nWe\u2019ve chosen the Taj M'Haul as our best deck bag because it supplies you with everything you might need from a great bag. It's fully waterproof so you can sling your phone, wallet, and camera in there with impunity. The super-durable 400-denier nylon shell means you won\u2019t have to worry about it taking a few scrapes and knocks. If it does end up in the water then there is no need to panic, as the whole bag floats! Its hi-viz striping will ensure that it is easy to recapture if it falls overboard, even in low light.\nAll these features, coupled with a spacious inner pocket and additional outer mesh pockets means you\u2019ll be able to carry all the gear you need in both style and complete functionality!"
"AsteraBox CRMX is the interface between lights and controller. It can be used as a wireless DMX transmitter with CRMX technology and also works as interface between app and lights. Its built-in battery enables an easy and time-saving setup, just like with all of Astera\u2019s products."
"The 1970\u2019s saw many social justice movements emerge, one of which was NALA, formally set up in 1980 by volunteers concerned at the lack of response to adults with literacy and numeracy difficulties in Ireland. They along with a small staff worked hard and smart in our first decade to set up Ireland\u2019s response to adult literacy. They launched a nationwide awareness raising campaign on adult literacy services, pioneered new teaching methods, created policy on good adult literacy work and organised events for adult literacy students and volunteer tutors.\nBy the mid 1990s, NALA concentrated on the professionalisation of the adult literacy service, with a focus on training and development. With this in train, the publication of the first Irish survey of adult literacy in 1997 provided an impetus to address adult literacy on a bigger scale. The survey showed that one in four adults had very low levels of literacy skills and a further 30% had below average skills. These findings were met with shock and disbelief. NALA's funding was increased and we intensified our efforts to get appropriate responses to adult literacy, involving providers of education and training and services working with the unemployed. We conducted research on the barriers facing adults with literacy difficulties in returning to learning, led a European project on quality assurance in adult literacy education and ran a vigorous pre-election campaign which got literacy on the political agenda. The first ever National Adult Literacy Programme (2000) was heavily influenced by NALA\u2019s work.\nThe 2000s were marked by our growing expertise in adult literacy, our adeptness at influencing adult literacy policy and our ability to lead and support good practice. We also launched one of the most internationally successful adult education projects ever, our TV series about adult literacy in RT\u00c9 One. Called Read Write Now it influenced thousands of people to return to education and was followed by 8 more TV series including The Really Useful Guide to Words and Numbers and more recently Written Off? During this time we supported thousands of adults learn by posting workbooks to them and providing tutor support over the phone and internet.\nSince it was established 30 years ago, NALA has been responsible for ensuring there is an awareness of the adult literacy issue in Ireland and a response to it from the state. The quality of that response and its growth has been at the core of our work to ensure people with literacy difficulties can avail of the tuition they want. Since 1997, NALA has been the dedicated force behind the 30 fold increase in adult literacy funding and the 10 fold increase in the number of people involved in tuition."
"Carol Smaul is the talented lady behind Gin & Griddle, a food and drink blog which won Food Review Blog of the Year 2017 in the Irish Blog Awards.\nThis Christmas we will be in California \u2013 LA, Santa Barbara, Palm Springs \u2013 before finishing in Seattle. So there\u2019s no doubt the wines we will be drinking and enjoying over Christmas and New Year will be from the US. We both love Californian wines, the big Cabernet Sauvignons, bold and smoky Zinfandels, lighter Pinot Noirs and buttery Chardonnays, all typical of the region.\nAs we\u2019ll be in Southern California we hope to try homegrown wines from Santa Barbara wine country as much as possible. In Santa Barbara, we plan to do the Urban Wine Trail, sampling wines from local wineries in the town. We\u2019re excited to discover new wines from this region and will certainly be buying a few bottles to enjoy over the rest of our holiday.\nCalifornian wine can be a little more challenging to locate in Ireland than its European counterparts, but it is definitely possible, once you\u2019re willing to pay a little more (excise and tax have not been kind to US wine in Ireland). Places such as Searsons, Baggot Street Wines, Blackrock Cellars and O\u2019Briens have reasonable selections and always ask in your local off licence or wine shop, particularly if they import their wine.\nOne of our favourite wines from the Southern California region is from Au Bon Climat, Pinot Noir. It would be a great choice for Christmas, an intense yet light red, with lots of character, earthy and fruity; an ideal accompaniment to the Turkey & Ham. The Au Bon Climat Santa Barbara Pinot Noir is currently in stock in Baggot Street Wines, priced at \u20ac37 (abv 13.5%)."
"Traveling is always amazing. You get the opportunity to meet new people, visit new places, and experience new cultures. Spending quality time on the road is an adventure that is full of joy, but there are also several challenges that you have to deal with. One of the most effective ways of traveling and enjoying life on the road is by living in a recreational vehicle (RV) that offers you the possibility to move around wherever you want. You are also free to take your spouse and kids with you and bring your belongings as well.\nHowever, things might be so tough when you are just getting started with RV travel. There is so much information that you will need to make good decisions that will make your RVing experience memorable. If you are a starter, then you are in the right place. We are here to offer you the best tips that will prepare you to hit the road and enjoy your RV camping. Read on to discover some of the top RV camping tips for beginners.\nWe can\u2019t deny the fact that there are lots of different types of RVs out there and choosing the best can be a daunting task to accomplish. You also have to decide whether you want to buy or rent your RV. For beginners, this isn\u2019t an easy decision to make with advantages and drawbacks for both of them. However, the moment you settle down and consider a few factors, it becomes easy to make a final decision.\nIf you are a starter, the best option is to rent until you decide the type of RV that suits your unique needs. Consider taking major road trips with different types of RVs and evaluate them critically to determine the best type that can satisfy your long-term camping goals and suit your style. Once you have taken several road trips and established what works for you, consider purchasing your own RV since it will save you a lot of money in the long run.\nWith little road experience, it is critical for RV beginners to take some time to learn how the motorhome works even if you are renting it. If something breaks unexpectedly, you should be in a good position to fix the mess and go on with your traveling and camping. This will save you a lot of money and time that you would have spent calling the mechanic.\nFurthermore, when you get t know your RV well, you are less likely to make costly operational errors. For instance, if you don\u2019t understand how many amps your primary breaker can handle, there is a high possibility that you will blow it off. This is a costly error that can easily be avoided by taking some time to understand your motorhome.\nAsk any experienced RVer out there, and he/she will tell you that the happiest RVers are those that have perfected the skill of managing their systems well. The prospect of your first time behind the RV wheel or your first time setting up a campsite and enjoying nature can be quite intimidating. However, going in with some advanced knowledge can easily turn the many firsts into successful events.\nThere is a plethora of blogs and videos that share the aspect of RV camping life are incredible sources of information that will guide you through your first steps of RV camping. There is a lot that you can learn which will help you to feel much comfortable and confident. Therefore, make sure that you watch the videos and read the blogs before you get started, and you will end up making a lot fewer mistakes as a newbie.\nAlthough driving a big vehicle for the first time might be intimidating, you need to do everything possible to get comfortable as a driver or a passenger. The best way to get rid of that fear before your camping day is by taking a practice RV drive. Keep in mind that you might appear \u2018taller\u201d and \u201cwider\u201d when driving an RV but that shouldn\u2019t bother you so much. Practice driving in wide and open spaces and watch for bridges, height clearances at underpasses, low-hanging tree branches and anything else that might block your way.\nYou should also consider taking some driving courses that will help you drive your recreational vehicle like a pro when you finally hit the road. As mentioned earlier, you should also take some time to know your RV and understand basic things about it. It will be awkward when you realize that it\u2019s too dark inside and you don\u2019t know where the light switch is located. You should also remember to secure all your personal belongings so that they don\u2019t shift while you are driving.\nTake care not to turn your RV into a moving auto parts store but don\u2019t forget to bring along some essential tools and spare parts that will come in handy when your vehicle breaks down unexpectedly.In most cases, the RVers are getting away from the busy city life and setting camps in quieter places. However, it is good to note that the further you get away from the city services, the more self-reliant you need to be.\nIt\u2019s a balance, and you need to remember the fact that each trip will always have its unique challenges, so it is critical to plan wisely before you hit the road. Some of the basic RV tools that you shouldn\u2019t miss to pack include; the digital tire gauges, heavy gauge jumper cables, and the T-handle style lug wrenches."
"South American Puffers are an active fish that can be easily kept in a community fish tank.\nThe South American Puffer, (scientific name Colomesus Asellus) grows up to only three inches in length when fully grown. They come from the upper regions of the Amazon River system and are a very active and extremely intelligent puffer. You will often see them buzzing about the tank, traveling up and down the glass. These puffer fish make a great freshwater addition to your aquarium.\nThe Amazon puffer needs to be kept in a tank that is at least 20 gallons. They seem to prefer a group, so a good number to have is three or more. Even so, some do well by themselves. They are not picky about the pH of the water and they prefer a temperature range of 74-80 degrees Fahrenheit. The tank should be heavily planted or decorated. You should also provide caves for the puffers to hang out in.\nThe South American Puffers are some of the most peaceful puffers. They can be kept with fast moving fish, including Zebras and Danios. Even though they are a peaceful fish, they should still not be kept with fish that have flowing fins as these fins tend to be too tempting for the puffer, and they will nip at them. Because of their peaceful nature, if you keep them in a heavily planted tank, the S. American Puffer can be a unique addition to a typical community fish tank.\nThese puffers must have a very crunchy diet to keep their teeth trimmed. You can feed them krill, mysis shrimp, bloodworms, blackworms, and plenty of snails. The snails should be fed several times a week. This puffer has very fast growing teeth and may require tooth trimming even with a high crunch diet. Because of how small the S. American Puffer is, and how active it is, you should feed this fish once or twice a day when you feed the rest of your community tank fish.\nKeeping the South American Puffer can be fun and quite entertaining. Be sure to set up a tank with plenty of decorations and plants for the puffer to explore. Understanding the proper care for this active fish can help you have a successful fresh water community aquarium."
"We have parents evening during the Autumn and Spring term where you will be able to find out about what your child is doing and how they are progressing towards their targets, children are invited along to the meetings to discuss the progress they have made. We regularly open our doors for parents to come and spend some time within school looking at the work their children have produced and generally celebrating success, we always encourage parental involvement in our school. We also provide opportunities for you to come into school and work with your child in their class. The school operates an open door policy whereby parents can pop in to speak to the class teacher- if they are not available then a meeting can be arranged. Formal assessments take place on a half-termly basis but if staff are concerned between these times they will approach parents. Home school books are used when it is beneficial and information is often added to reading record books. Individual Provision Map\u2019s (IPM\u2019s) are reviewed and updated on a half-termly basis and signed by the teacher, parent and child (where appropriate). All pupils with SEND should make at least expected progress, in line with their peers."
