"The Renault Kadjar (2018 petrol manual) has been named February\u2019s fastest selling used car, flying off forecourts in an average of just 17 days.\nProving that sports utility vehicles (SUV) are fast becoming the UK\u2019s favourite, this is the third consecutive month that an SUV has taken the top spot as the fastest selling used car. And consumers are happy to splash out on their perfect 4x4 \u2013 the Mercedes-Benz GLC took the top spot in December and January, and in February featured in both sixth and seventh place.\nBig was clearly best regionally too, with the body type taking the top spot in London (2015 Nissan Qashqai petrol automatic - 37 days), Northern Ireland (2016 Hyundai Tucson diesel manual \u2013 36 days), Scotland (2018 Renault Kadjar petrol manual \u2013 32 days), South (2016 Hyundai Tucson diesel manual \u2013 56 days), South East (2018 Mercedes-Benz GLC-Class diesel automatic \u2013 34 days) and Wales (2018 Nissan Qashqai diesel manual \u2013 30 days).\nElsewhere, February was Ford\u2019s month with the marque\u2019s motors shifting quickest in the East Midlands (2016 Ford Focus petrol manual \u2013 52 days), North East (2016 Ford Fiesta petrol manual - 73 days), South West (2013 Ford Fiesta petrol manual \u2013 36 days), West Midlands (2012 Ford Focus petrol manual \u2013 34 days) and Yorkshire (2012 Ford Focus diesel manual - 31 days).\nKarolina Edwards-Smajda, Auto Trader\u2019s Director of Commercial Products, said: \u201cAside from the clear signs of a huge investment in electric, this month\u2019s Geneva Motor Show was dominated by an exciting array of new generation 4x4s and crossovers. And with SUVs taking the crown for overall fastest selling used car for three consecutive months, their position as a forecourt favourite shows no sign of abating.\nHitting targets will always be determined by how quickly a retailer can turn their stock around and using data is the key to speeding it up. Users of i-Control, Auto Trader\u2019s data management solution, took an average of just 39 days to sell a car in February, compared to a market average of 74; 47% faster than those that don\u2019t use the data management tool."
"In an ideal location close to 502 and the Leisure Complex, this home unit offers low cost, low maintenance living with supplementary solar power, ducted evaporative cooling and gas heating. There are two bedrooms with robes, a separate lounge and good dining area. The kitchen has ample cupboards, gas bench hot plates, wall oven and a dishwasher. Separate bathroom with shower, vanity and toilet. Separate laundry and an outdoor living area which takes full advantage of the views. There is also a single carport and a handy garden shed."
"Creativity is a tricky thing to learn and perhaps even harder to teach. What works for one may make no sense to another. That goes for students and tutors. Perhaps the best way to address this is to get stuck in, make stuff, and then try and work out what happened.\nJoin OCA tutors Bryan Eccleshall (Painting/Drawing) and Priscilla Edwards (Textiles) for a day long workshop at Sheffield\u2019s Millennium Galleries designed to help students of all levels and across all disciplines explore how ideas can be generated through a process of making and remaking.\nUsing work displayed at the Graves and Millennium Galleries as starting points you will explore and develop practical strategies for making work. Your initial response may be to draw, write, take photographs, or even shoot films. You will then develop this raw material on your own or in collaboration with others as the day progresses. There will also be short illustrated talks by Priscilla and Bryan to help you along.\nAlthough this workshop encourages you to draw, you will be free to photograph, build, trace, sew, argue, discuss, film, and generally mess around with the implications of what is produced. It is hoped that you will leave with an idea of how revisiting work and folding it into other work \u2013 perhaps someone else\u2019s \u2013 can enrich the making experience. You may not finish anything, but you should end up with some interesting questions.\nYou are also encouraged to bring examples of your own work to use source material. It would be especially interesting to discuss unfinished or unsatisfactory work that still fascinates or bothers you.\nSome materials will be provided, but feel free to bring anything you think you might need. Be prepared to make a bit of a mess and dress appropriately.\nThis workshop is suitable for students studying at any level as it is designed for students to build on their skill base already developed through prior learning. Invention and openness to experimentation is the focus, not technical ability.\nVenue: The Activity Room close the Millennium Galleries in central Sheffield.\nWe have the Activity Room from 10am \u2013 5pm. Activities will get going in earnest at 10.30am and we will finish at 4pm, which gives us a chance to clear up.\nPlaces are limited to 23 participants.\nThere are coffee shops / shops nearby, but you may want to bring a packed lunch. There will not be a formal break.\n\u2190 Documentary evidence and artistic expression.\nDocumentary evidence and artistic expression."
"Abstract : Touchscreen interaction currently relies on a limited set of multi-touch gestures and a wide range of graphical widgets that are often difficult to manipulate and consume much screen real-estate. Many tasks remain tedious to perform on touchscreens: selecting text over multiple views, manipulating different degrees of freedom of a graphical object, invoking a command and setting its parameter values in a row. We propose a design space of simple multi-touch gestures that designers of user interfaces can systematically explore to propose more gestures to users. We further consider a set of 32 gestures for tablet-sized devices, by proposing an incremental recognition engine that works with current hardware technology, and empirically testing the usability of those gestures. In our experiment, individual gestures are recognized with an average accuracy of ~90\\%, and users successfully achieve some of the transitions between gestures without the use of explicit delimiters. The goal of our contribution is to assist designers in optimizing the use of the rich multi-touch input channel for the activation of discrete and continuous controls, and enable fluid transitions between controls."
