"As this page suggests we are, retired, dedicated volunteers serving the Virginia Beach Retired Employees Association. Strong work ethics saw us through our careers with the city and we continue our dedication as a member of the VBREA.\nRetirement does have its advantages\u2026like leisurely enjoying the boardwalk whether strolling, biking, or riding in a cart\u2026we can make the time happen because YOU are your Clock Master.\nOur association was started in 2006 at the request of then City Manager, Mr. James K. Spore as a city partnership under the guidance of the city's Administrative Leadership Team.\nA mailbox located within the city has been dedicated exclusively for VBREA usage, a savings/banking account was established, monthly meetings were established and a unique contribution number was assigned to the VBREA for the United Way annual contributions.\nThe existence of this association is intended to keep retired members informed of current city business and to help retirees be better aware of current volunteer opportunities within the city.\nThe mission of the association is to enhance the quality of life for its members. The association organizes activities of special interest to retirees, establishes and maintains a liaison with appropriate city staff to assure retiree interests are being recognized, and provides a channel of communication through which interest and concerns can be represented to the city administration.\nBelow is the VBREA Structural Diagram. As indicated, the President/Executive Director oversees the Webmaster and three (3) additional Executive Directors as well as five (5) At-large Directors.\nEach of the five (5) At-large Board Members acts as liaison between the board body and the respective committees identified below. The At-large Director may, or may not, additionally serve as the Lead for a committee.\nGeneral membership are encouraged to serve on their desired committee. The member need only step-forward by contacting any board member to volunteer. Serving is highly recommended and appreciated.\n\u200bThe VBREA Duties and Responsibilities - Guidelines for Directors, Committees, and the Webmaster identifies each position, duties, tasks, and points of contact related to those actions.\nClick the image to the right to download the VBREA Duties and Responsibilities - Guidelines.\nClick the image below to download the updated VBREA Bylaws.\nThe bylaws have been revised and approved effective, October 18, 2017.\nThe original, fully executed, hard-copy document is filed with the board's formal papers. Download a copy by clicking on the file to the right.\n\u200bVBREA Directors Board Meetings are held monthly on the third Wednesday, or as needed, at the City of Virginia Beach Municipal Center in the Agriculture Building, #14 beginning at 10:00 a.m.\nThe VBREA directorship positions exists to monitor and guide membership while serving under the association Bylaws.\nThe Involvement Committee meets to coordinate VBREA activities on the first Tuesday of each month at the Municipal Center in the Agriculture Building, #14 beginning at 10:00 a.m.\nThe Communications Committee publishes a quarterly newsletter and communicates applicable retiree news to our membership along with an array of additional responsibilities promoting communications. The committee meets on the second Tuesday of the month beginning at 9:30 a.m.\ncommittees? We look forward to hearing from you."
"Working at Hodo - Ana Munoz \u2014 Always Plant-Based. Always Delicious. Hodo.\nHodo was selected to be featured in this year\u2019s Bay Area Urban Manufacturing Initiative (BAUMI) with a story highlighting our team member, Ana Munoz. Read her story below.\nAna Munoz started working at Hodo \u2013 a thriving producer of organic plant-based foods in Oakland \u2013 as part of the entry-level production team in 2013. After 2 years, Ana left her job at Hodo to give birth to her third child. While Ana enjoyed working at Hodo, she decided to take a break from work to raise her children and spend time with her family.\nAfter seven months raising her newborn, Ana missed the work and her co-workers and decided to return. Hodo welcomed Ana back to her old position and she was impressed to see how much growth had taken place at the company. Hodo had gone from a roughly 20-person business when Ana first started to now employing over 120 people, with distribution expanding Nationwide.\nWith Hodo\u2019s success, Ana found that opportunities abounded. Since she first started at the company, Hodo has trained her in various departments, including packaging and, upon her return, kitchen production. Just two years after her return, Ana was promoted to Kitchen Lead, preparing Hodo\u2019s line of ready-to-eat products.\n\u201cWe\u2019re always learning a lot here. I didn\u2019t know anything about tofu or yuba. I didn\u2019t know anything about food safety or working safely. Now, I have all those skills \u2013 preparation, documentation, following GMP protocols and recipes to produce various products in batches.\u201d Ana also treasures the opportunity her job creates for her children. When she immigrated from Mexico as a teenager, Ana had a tough time adjusting to high school, to the culture, to the language. Today, she speaks with confidence and supervises a production shift at Hodo.\nLaunched at the 2016 Bay Area Urban Manufacturing Summit, the Bay Area Urban Manufacturing Initiative (BAUMI) is a three-year project to facilitate the creation of a well-defined and interconnected regional manufacturing ecosystem in the Bay Area. Learn more about BAUMI. Hodo is a proud supporter of BAUMI and company in West Oakland."
