"How Good is Your Ministry's Team Work? Take This Test to Find Out.\nHow well does your children's ministry team work together? Take this test to find out. One answer per question.\nTeam members are provided feedback about their performance.\nTeam members work together for the common good of the organization.\nThere are many complaints and morale is low on the team.\nTeam members don't understand the decisions that are made and or don't agree with them.\nTeam members are encouraged to be good team members and build relationships.\nTeam members are provided with growth opportunities.\nMeetings are inefficient and there is a lot of overlap.\nTeam members are asked to commit to the team's values and leaders help them see how their role fits in.\nTeam members are often given a chance to work on ideas that stretch their thinking and ideas.\nThe team understands what it is supposed to accomplish and has the resources needed to be successful.\nConflict between team members is a pervasive issue.\nTeam members feel that good work is not rewarded and they are not sure what they are responsible for.\nTeam members balance the need for autonomy with mutual dependance.\nWorking relationships are poor and there is a lack of commitment for the vision.\nWe spend time together as a team outside of work.\n46 to 60 - You're a solid team working well together.\n31 - 45 - Your effectiveness as a team is patchy. You're good at some things but need improvement in other areas.\n0 - 30 - This is something to be concerned about. The good news is you've got lots of room for improvement.\nI believe this survey needs to be revised, it favors many of the things that make a team unsuccessful. It does not differentiate between high scores of great qualities or high scores of poor characteristics."
"This is a drop from Coren Direbrew, the boss in Blackrock Depths, during the Brewfest world event.\nThe 2009 Brewfest version of Sundial of the Exiled.\nDrops from Coren Direbrew, in the Grim Guzzler inside BRD.\nCurrently DOES stack with Abyssal Rune. Makes for some pretty painful crits.\nNo flavour text for spellcasters this year too?\nThis trinket will sometimes proc this spell.\nIt has the same benefits as Sundial of the Exiled.\nDid test it today ..\nTo calculate equivalent of proc in spellpower will modify formula copied from Sundial of the Exiled.\nJust go this trinket yesterday. OMG it rocks! 4 ticks into combustion with no crits yet this thing procs. As soon as I saw it proc I popped Cannoneer's Fuselighter and start casting fireball. 9K! Cast another fireball. 9K! I'm 1/4 of the way through the cast of the 3rd fireball when hot streak also pops! I send in the 3rd fireball with a pyroblast 1/2 second behind it.... 10K! ... 12K!\nI am using this on my Holy Pally. I Judge every 8 seconds, so I am hoping that it procs often....not sure, anyone care to share thoughts?\nYou should reroll arcane blast spam or frostfire bolt spam.\nQuestion for all you number crunchers: I have Abyssal Rune, Mithril Pocketwatch, and Darkmoon Card: Death. What is the best pair for dps i.e. PvE. Is there an advantage to another pairing in PvP?\nThe tooltip states that it procs from harmful spells, but it's been proccing when I use Power Word: Shield in my Shadow spec. Most likely a bug, but I found it interesting (and useful!).\nIt's strange because Abyssal Rune, which has identical stats except haste <> crit, can proc at the same time as Sundial of the Exiled!\nPerfect combo btw, in a serious bossfight both trinkets can give you +1180 SP more than once (you will proc 590SP 20% of the casts, and 1180SP 4% of the casts) So if your fight takes 4 minutes, you'll have an average of 19 procs, some of them will proc at the same time. That grants 11210SP over 240sec, so 46,7SP overall.\nCuriously, this procs from Sacred Shield.\nEquip: Your harmful spells have a chance to increase your spell power by 175 for 10 sec."
"On Tuesday 20 March, the Lions Club of Sanctuary Cove Hope Island held the 2nd Annual Raewyn Andersen Memorial Golf Day at the Palms Golf Course, Sanctuary Cove. Registration for the event was from 11.00am with a shotgun tee off at 12.00 midday with Golfers playing a 4 Ball Ambrose Competition. Once golf was done and dusted, everyone enjoyed dinner at the Sanctuary Cove Rec Club where prizes were awarded, and raffles and auctions contributed to the fundraising effort.\nRaewyn Andersen was a charter member of the Lions Club of Sanctuary Cove, avid golfer and a character of the Cove. Everyone knew Raewyn. She was also a great supporter of Lions Medical Research Foundation and in 2012, at the age of 67 was an entrant in the Qld and Northern NSW Lions Medical Research Personality Quest raising thousands of dollars for medical research.\nSadly, Raewyn passed away two years ago after a relatively short time from an aggressive cancer. In her memory, her club agreed to hold an annual charity golf day with 50% of the proceeds going to her favourite charity, Lions Medical Research Foundation, and the other half to a recognized charity of their choice.\nIn 2017 the event was a great success raising $7,500 which was donated to a local children\u2019s charity and Lions Medical Research Foundation. In 2018, the event attracted great support from the local community and the Club was able to raise almost double last year\u2019s amount \u2013 again, half will go to Lions Medical Research Foundation.\nSincerest thanks to the Lions Club of Sanctuary Cove Hope Island. Their commitment and dedication to honouring a charter member of their Club while fundraising for worthy causes, is exceptional.\nPhoto (L to R) Incoming President Peter Spence, Narelle Parkins (LMRF), President Sally Baumber and Sanctuary Cove Hope Island Lions club member Barb Campbell."
