ide to the Doberman Pinscher. United States: Dace Publishing. 1997. pp. 9\u201311.\n^ a b \"Breed history\". Dobermann Pinscher Club of America. Retrieved 24 August 2016.\n^ \"Most Popular Dog Breeds\".\n^ \"Doberman Gang, The\". Monthly Film Bulletin. 41 (480): 174. January 1974.\n^ Dogspot. \"Doberman Dog Breed Information\".\n^ \"Graf Belling v. Gr\u00f6nland\". Doberman Pedigrees. Retrieved 13 August 2010.\n^ Locke, Michelle. \"DOBERMAN HEROES OF WORLD WAR II\". Doberman Rescue Unlimited. Archived from the original on 28 November 2014. Retrieved 28 November 2014.\n^ Doberman Pinscher. Kennel Club Books. 2008. p. 15. ISBN 978-1-59378-230-6.\n^ \"Bingo von Ellendonk\". Doberman Pedigrees. Retrieved 13 August 2010.\n^ \"Borong the Warlock\". Retrieved 8 August 2010.\nWikimedia Commons has media related to Dobermann."
"The Centre for Indigenous Initiatives offers Kin\u00e0m\u00e0gawin workshops and training sessions to members of the Carleton community upon request. Kin\u00e0m\u00e0gawin workshops provide a safe space for the university community to voice their experiences, share their stories, learn new things and practice skills. Workshops are available through the Educational Resource Centre; in classrooms at the request of professors; and to other groups on campus who do related work.\nWorkshops are facilitated by the Centre for Indigenous Initiatives staff and, as often as possible, Carleton students.\nThe length of a workshop is a minimum of 1.5 hours. That time typically includes viewing the 42-minute film, a discussion with participants, and a participatory activity.\nFor more information and/or to schedule a Kin\u00e0m\u00e0gawin workshop, fill out the Contact Form online or call (613) 520-5622."
