"Astrology and tarot cards for the week 30 June \u2013 6 July 2014.\nA lot of heavenly bodies (planets and luminaries) change house this week. It means the energies shift into different areas of your life, changing the focus about somewhat.\nThe focus today is very much on your public life. The Moon is in Leo in the 11th house with a lot of beneficial activity in the 10th house via Jupiter, the Sun and Mercury. Uranus in Aries moves from the 8th into the 7th house.\nPluto in Capricorn in the 4th astrological house opposes the Sun in Cancer for about a week and starts to be felt strongly today, reaching its peak on the 4th of July. (Independence day for American viewers, hooray! \ud83d\ude42 There may be a sense of, you vs. others and how your identity is felt. Ask yourself, what is it I want to do today?\nSaturn in Scorpio moves houses, expect more structure, predictability and reliability in your finances.\nMercury is out of retrograde for the 1st full day now and moves into the 9th house of learning. Communication really skyrockets!\nThe Moon is at 0 degrees Libra near the astrological Virgo Ascendant. You will feel a desire to create, harmonise and to be inspired! It\u2019s a good time to take action.\nThe 1st quarter Moon occurs and Mars changes house position into the 1st house. The creation of something amazing within you has begun!\nThe Mars \u2013 Moon astrology theme continues. Whether the project that is being created (on an inner or outer level) know it will be wonderful and will serve you in a practical sense.\nThe Devil (Twin Peaks Tarot) \u2013 Aries will find the weekend a wonderfully creative time. You\u2019ll feel your inner spark strongly and will be able to use it to achieve a tangible result.\nThe Emperor (Tarot of the Pirates) \u2013 Sagittarius \u2013 You may resist a sense of financial limitation, especially around Wednesday.\nThe Hermit (Tarot of the Trees) \u2013 Pisces \u2013 You may feel a strain in asserting yourself in tricky family situations on Tuesday."
"Further enforcing the point that his time with One Direction was one entirely bereft of joy and good times, shade king Zayn Malik admits he wanted out of the group from the start.\nZayn -- whose immaculately crafted face is so perfectly symmetrical, so overwhelming in its otherworldliness, it could make a believer out of even the most stringent atheist -- quickly realized the group's bubblegum pop (which eventually evolved into classic rock-inspired dad jams) wasn't the right fit for him.\nKicking things off with a 1D-related quip, he said, \"I realized the direction we were going in -- mind the pun -- with the music, I instantly knew it wasn't for me because I realized I couldn't put any input in.\"\nRather than fight the rest of the group on their sound, Zayn chose to go with the flow instead: \"You kind of just go with whatever's going on, and I didn't want to be the person to say 'I was right' because who am I to say [that]?\"\nStill, Zayn says, the rest of the group engaged his vocal style as best they could: \"I think the boys kind of knew that wasn't my cup of tea...They would try to facilitate certain things for me so I could sing certain R&B ad-libs here and there, and that was nice.\"\nWhen it was time for Zayn to quit, though, the rest of the group didn't put up much of a fight.\n\"They obviously didn't want me to leave,\" he said. \"But they couldn't talk me out of it at that point. I'd already made my mind up.\"\nSo it is that Zayn is a solo act, so it is that One Direction have succumbed to the infamous boyband hiatus.\nZayn's first official single \"Pillow Talk\" doesn't drop until Friday (January 29), but be sure to tune for his full interview with Zane Lowe when it airs on Apple Beats 1 Radio tomorrow at 12PM EST."
"Moxibustion is a form of heat therapy that originated from China more than 2500 years ago. The goal of moxibustion is to balance the body, ensure consistency of airflow and achieve equilibrium. The therapy is carried out by burning \u201cmoxa\u201d (dried plant materials) near the skin\u2019s surface. This powerful method of using herbs has a long history that dates back to ancient China and medieval Europe, but still used in civil societies to relieve stomach pain, irregular menstruation, anxiety and itching of the skin. However, although moxibustion is beneficial to the human body, it has its drawbacks. SMARTMOXA is designed to improve traditional ways of practising moxibustion. It does not require any burning of \u201cmoxa\u201d, where users can burn themselves by accident, cause fire hazards, or produce lots of smoke. By replacing the need for traditional \u201cmoxa\u201d sticks, it makes moxibustion safer and more convenient for everyone. To use SMARTMOXA, simply add high-purity \u201cmoxa\u201d into the device and turn on the tail cap switch to get the device going. The high-purity \u201cmoxa\u201d is heated to 250 degrees Celsius, the optimum temperature to send energy into the human body. The device\u2019s 3200mAh battery allows the entire equipment system to run continuously for 90 minutes, where a moxibustion process usually runs for 5 to 10 minutes at a time."
"A fast-changing environment forces farmers to think differently.\nAfter his first two sentences, I knew that Joel Yiri from Jirapa was the man I was looking for. I had asked Peter Kuupenne, an extension officer from Ghana\u2019s Ministry of Food and Agriculture, to meet \u201ca creative farmer\u201d. And that is what I found: Joel was a man with a vision.\nFrom that moment on, I just knew it was going to be an interesting testimonial as part of the series we were recording for the Adaptation and Mitigation Knowledge Network project.\nJoel inherited his plot of land from his father, but the soil had become infertile. For years he was one of the many migrant workers who go south to farm on other people\u2019s land. \u201cBut I realised I was wasting my energy. I work on someone else\u2019s land to earn money and buy food. Why not farm my own plots?\u201d.\nEvery year the rains have started later and stopped earlier. To make matters worse, the first rains are often followed by a drought, which sometimes lasts for a month. That\u2019s why Joel is looking further ahead: \u201cI want to take a micro-finance loan to dig a borehole on my land. If I have access to water I could grow vegetables even during the dry season. A borehole with a simple irrigation system would cost about US$7000.\u201d It\u2019s a considerable investment, but Joel is sure he could pay back the loan in two years.\n\u201cAn NGO came to look at my plot. They found termite heaps and several indigenous scrubs indicating there is water nearby, so they won\u2019t have to dig deep. My plot is ready. With my 65 pigs I have enough manure to treat that surface, so the only thing I now need is water. It would also benefit the other people on the nearby plots.\n\u201cFarmers should be thinking of the future,\u201d Joel concludes, \u201cWe should farm differently from our grandfathers. They took to farming only as a way to survive \u2013 to eat. If a crop failed, the next year they would try the same thing again, and again.\nThe way ahead for researchers and smallholder farmers facing current and coming challenges posed by climate change lies in learning from each other while sharing knowledge and experiences.\nThe Adaptation and Mitigation Knowledge Network (AMKN) is a central place for accessing and sharing current agricultural adaptation and mitigation knowledge. It illustrates farmers\u2019 realities on the ground and links them with promising scientific research outputs to inspire new ideas and highlight the current challenges that need to be tackled to improve climate change resilience and the livelihoods of smallholder farmers.\nThe AMKN is an initiative of the Climate Change Agriculture and Food Security (CCAFS) program.\nFirst published online for the Climate Change Agriculture and Food Security Program (http://www.ccafs.cgiar.org) and the Adaptation and Mitigation Knowledge Network (http://amkn.org) projects of the Consultative Group on International Agricultural Research. Reproduced with permission."
"NAGURU \u2013 Days after the close of the January transfer window, Police FC have unveiled five new additions to their squad.\nKey among the new players that were shown to the Media at the club\u2019s head quarters in Naguru was former Proline and Kaizer Chiefs defender Ivan Bukenya.\nBukenya joins the Cops from Swedish third tier side, Linkoping FC for whom he has been playing since early last year. He is expected to feature for the Cops until the end of the season.\nAt the unveiling, Police FC chairperson Asan Kasingye told the media that the club\u2019s aim it to compete for the league title.\n\u201cThe purpose of this recruitment is to improve the team\u2019s performance as we should be competing for the title not just to participate as we have been in the league for the last three decades, said Kasingye.\n\u201cHowever, we are hoping to improve on our staff, improve on our playground and work on players\u2019 remuneration as we expect the players to deliver.\nThe other new players include defender Edgar Sharp Bwogi who last season featured at Express FC, Kasozi Kennedy from Vipers (loan), Alfred Oginyera and Kiryowa Gerard who both join from Farmers FC in the regional league.\nKiryowa and Bwogi signed one year deals, each while goalkeeper Oginyera put pen to paper on a two year contract and will deputise Davis Mutebi who has featured in all of Police\u2019s first 16 games this season.\nPolice are set to take on Express FC today (Monday) at Wankulukuku in their 17th League game of the season.\nThey are currently 10th in the League standings with 21 points so far."
